window.harnessChunks["rows-c08281322cc3102e.js"]=["<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-29T20:18:59+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/graniet/kheish\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Kheish</a><small>2024</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-29\">2026-10-29</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/graniet/kheish/tree/df50433f8d577f832c6812b9e67be8ddb766059d\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision df50433f8d577f832c6812b9e67be8ddb766059d\">df50433 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: perform user/project tasks by model-selected tool actions, revisions and outputs through durable agent sessions. - Disturbance / variety regulated: heterogeneous goals, environmental/tool feedback, interrupted processes, provider failures and variable execution steps. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose next task/tool action after model output and previous observation, or request further help within the session scope. - Decision owner: configured model-backed root and sidechain agents; the daemon enforces execution, persistence and credentials rather than selecting task-specific solutions. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: AgentRuntime, AgentOrchestrator, scoped ToolRuntime, permission engine, run journals and resume queues. - Closure path: submitted session input → model-selected call → tool result or gate/interrupt → persisted observation/resumed turn → model revises or finishes. - Boundary reachability: daemon serve plus configured provider and normal session/run API create the shipped execution path; sidechains and their narrowed context are spawned by first-party supervisor APIs. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: open-ended tool and next-step selection rests with the model loop; fixed queues and policy merely limit that discretion. - Evidence: README quickstart; runtime.rs, orchestrator.rs, supervisor.rs; docs/concepts/agents-and-sidechains.mdx. - Basis: structural model/tool/observation lifecycle and explicit supported daemon entry. - Confidence: high for architecture, not for live task performance. - Caveats: provider credentials are required for a real model run; no paid-provider task was executed here.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: C - Function: attenuate duplicate execution of one shared project commitment and invalid concurrent starts while separate session agents pursue project work. - Disturbance / variety regulated: more than one actor attempting to start the same assigned project task, stale assignment during preparation, or a dependent work cell starting before its predecessor&#39;s completion. - Decisive decision or feedback right: admit only a task start whose assignee/dependencies are still valid, and reject a second nonterminal start for that shared commitment. - Decision owner: constructor-authored project service reservation/dependency rules, not the model that submits a start request. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: durable project/member/task store, blockedby, expected-assignee compare, latest-run correlation, and session run queue. - Closure path: shared task state → reservation/revalidation → one eligible assignee run starts or competing/unready request is rejected → later project task state and dependency completion determine subsequent starts. - Boundary reachability: project service and start API are shipped daemon features and use the same persisted normal session-run path; no external scheduler or ad hoc user lock is needed. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: rule-based start arbitration is constructor-owned; the project coordinator can still choose work, but these collision gates do not delegate the arbitration rule to a model. - Distinct S1 units: separately configured project session members, each with a managed model/tool runtime; a project task has one assignee session. - Inter-S1 disturbance: simultaneous attempts to act on the same project task and downstream work using unfinished or reassigned output risk duplicate/conflicting execution. - Attenuating coordination relation: atomic start reservation, nonterminal duplicate-start rejection, assignment revalidation and completed-dependency requirement. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: rejected starts do not generate a second run; successful run state updates the project task so dependent session work can become eligible. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: the gates handle a concrete shared-work collision and prerequisite disturbance across separately executing sessions, changing actual run eligibility, not merely moving messages. - Evidence: services/project.rs reservation and dependency tests; state/projectworkflow.rs; docs/concepts/projects-and-project-tasks.mdx. - Basis: structural concurrency control in first-party daemon code. - Confidence: medium; bounded to project-task start contention and declared dependencies. - Caveats: this does not prove general conflict avoidance for agents editing the same external file or that every project elects this mode.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: monitor and revise current project/session work commitments across delegated task agents. - Disturbance / variety regulated: missed subtasks, changing current priorities, failed children, assignment bottlenecks and incomplete work dependencies. - Decisive decision or feedback right: the configured coordinating model chooses subtasks/owners, reads current status/results, and updates or reassigns active commitments through control tools. - Decision owner: model executing the first-party coordination specialist profile at the declared group level; a human/project operator can separately direct or override it, but ordinary model-controlled current work is the credited mode. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: listtasks, updatetask, child spawn/messaging, stored project/session task state, permission and dependency gates; the daemon enforces what the agent chooses. - Closure path: coordinator sees active task/child state → directs or revises assignment and follow-up → service validates/persists change → affected session agent receives task/mailbox work or subsequently eligible project start. - Boundary reachability: coordinator profile is shipped in helpers.rs and is executable as a managed agent with exposed task and sidechain control tools; its decisions affect the same daemon sessions/project execution boundary. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the model, not the deterministic task store, decides which commitment needs revision; the store implements authorized state change. - Whole-system current view: the selected project or session group&#39;s task list, assignees, dependency/status records and returned child snapshots/messages, not a claim to control unrelated daemon tenants. - Current-control decision scope: active group task decomposition, ownership/status revisions, follow-up and interruption/stop within configured capability scope; no ultimate organizational policy authority. - Evidence: controltools/helpers.rs coordinator instructions; controltools/tasks.rs list/update, orchestration and mailboxes; services/project.rs task state; docs/concepts/agents-and-sidechains.mdx. - Basis: explicit packaged model role plus the corresponding executable state/feedback tools. - Confidence: medium; reachable configurable coordinator mode, not a demonstrated unattended deployment. - Caveats: external model/provider and appropriate rights are required; a simple single-session deployment may not exercise group-level S3.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: complementary independent challenge to ordinary operating/current-control reports, beyond routine task checking. - Disturbance / variety regulated: inaccurate agent self-reporting, concealed execution defects and misleading task completion claims. - Decisive decision or feedback right: an independently sourced audit judgment that can challenge current organizational control. - Decision owner: no sufficiently independent shipped audit decision owner established for the declared group; ordinary run and Flow reviewers remain part of configured task execution. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: append-only event journals, signed external-action records, debug captures, evidence-required Flow projections and optional verification sidechains. - Closure path: review/evidence may fail a Flow or send a child finding to its parent task, but no distinct complementary audit channel with separate access and S3 correction is mandated. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: logs, routine review and separation of a tool profile are not by themselves independent organizational audit judgment. - Evidence: supervisor.rs audit entries; docs/concepts/playbooks-and-flows.mdx; docs/concepts/agents-and-sidechains.mdx. - Basis: boundary and independence comparison. - Confidence: medium-high within sampled shipped paths. - Caveats: an application may compose its own truly independent auditor, but that is not attributed to the default first-party group.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: prospective intelligence that models the changing external environment, forms adaptation options and returns them to current capability. - Disturbance / variety regulated: future provider/ecosystem changes, changing requirements and long-horizon operational risks. - Decisive decision or feedback right: evaluate future environmental possibilities and authorize candidate capability adaptations. - Decision owner: no shipped first-party prospectively oriented S4 owner established; optional model learning judges and operators review past run knowledge. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: semantic fact/preference/decision extraction, scoped retrieval, optional judge, verified publication tiers and reviewed procedure skill rollout. - Closure path: terminal run → learning candidate/review → future prompt memory or separate operator skill promotion; this is retrospective knowledge reuse, not an external-prospective S3–S4 conversation. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: adaptive memory and procedure activation can change later prompts, but no distinct future-environment model and option-return loop was found. - Evidence: docs/concepts/semantic-learning-and-procedural-skills.mdx; services/learning.rs, services/learningpolicy.rs and services/learningjudge.rs. - Basis: documented feature modes contrasted against Profile prospective requirement. - Confidence: medium-high for reviewed first-party learning modes. - Caveats: users may supply a future-scanning agent as an application, not a credited bundled organizational S4.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: legitimate ultimate identity/mission/policy adjudication at the project/daemon recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: contested operating purpose, credential risk, permission exceptions and conflicts between operational need and identity boundaries. - Decisive decision or feedback right: settle a genuinely identity-level issue and return an authoritative policy decision to govern subsequent organization. - Decision owner: external operator/installer defines purpose, permissions, provider credentials and runtime configuration; no internally reachable identity-policy decision and return cycle is established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: brokered auth leases, inherited non-widening child scopes, approval/user-question APIs, static runtime policy, Playbook release states and audit trail. - Closure path: an operator can configure/approve a task and execution resumes, but the reviewed standard product does not distinguish and route an ultimate identity issue through first-party S5 closure. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: permission and human-input gates restrict individual actions; they are not authority to redefine the system&#39;s identity or settle S3–S4 tensions. - Evidence: README security; docs/concepts/approvals-and-questions.mdx, docs/runtime/permissions.mdx, docs/concepts/playbooks-and-flows.mdx and runtime auth/permission code. - Basis: explicit operator-rights boundary and absence of a policy-level escalation contract. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: a wider organization deploying Kheish can supply its own legitimate parent S5, but no separate first-party P loop is evidenced at this chosen boundary.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/ba989f8be97bd7c592b6e9e969e185562b9814d4/assessments/kh/kheish.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit ba989f8be97bd7c592b6e9e969e185562b9814d4\">ba989f8 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-29T20:07:55+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/tcztzy/swarmx\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">SwarmX</a><small>2024</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-29\">2026-10-29</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/tcztzy/swarmx/tree/a171e0da84c7a4d667d2c314cfbed66847f3040b\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision a171e0da84c7a4d667d2c314cfbed66847f3040b\">a171e0d &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Produce goal-directed work through native agent tool loops and retained responsibility for delegated results. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Task-specific uncertainty, failing/partial child results, native errors and differing capabilities. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Select operational actions, delegation and corrective follow-up until a goal is ready for independent acceptance or escalation. - Decision owner: The configured native executor or managed supervisor Agent; the user separately owns criteria and final acceptance. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: ProductServices native adapters, recorded execution, preparation checks, WorkManager reservations and cancellation propagation. - Closure path: runWork prepares goal/criteria → native supervisor/executor → child/tool results → agent judges next operational action → returned output/submission. - Boundary reachability: runWork explicitly injects managed supervisor instructions and dispatches configured native Agents; source exposes and checks swarm.prepare/sendmessage. Native dependencies and credentials are explicit supported setup, not borrowed developer agents. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The Host does not invent the next corrective message; removing the native agent&#39;s judgments leaves dispatch/recording without the operational response choices. - Evidence: apps/desktop/src/host/product-services.ts lines 531–609 and 800–891; packages/core/swarm/skills/delegate/SKILL.md; docs/native-agents.md. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high for the wired supported mode. - Caveats: No live native model/provider run was executed. Operational completion is not independent acceptance.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: No material first-party function established at the declared recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Interference between concurrent work agents or review consumers. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No function-specific decisive-return path supplied. - Decision owner: No qualifying owner; operational agent choices, caller settings and runtime enforcement remain distinct. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Cycle concurrency/reservations and directory-scoped review locks attenuate concrete duplicate consumption and overspending, but the response is fixed Host/SQLite enforcement; no first-party agent coordination decision or S2-specific autonomous composition contract is exposed. - Closure path: Positive, function-specific constructor and eligible parent-mode alternatives checked; no qualifying closure remains. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The bounded absence concerns the organizational relation, not implementation purity. - Evidence: apps/desktop/src/host/work.ts; docs/memory.md; apps/desktop/tests/memory-review-ownership.test.ts - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high within the assessed distribution. - Caveats: A custom deployment could add the function; that is outside this shipped boundary.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-p\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: P - Function: Regulate current whole-cycle commitments, resources and accountability across persistent work items. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Competing goals, held/spent/unknown costs, unresolved effects, limited capacity and changing accepted commitments. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Set/revise cycle budget and goal criteria, start/stop/revise work, reconcile unresolved outcomes/invoices and record independent acceptance. - Decision owner: The legitimate local operator through trusted desktop/Host administration; active Agents are explicitly denied this authority. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: WorkManager transactions, expected-version checks, retained reservations, descendant accounting and authenticated window IPC. - Closure path: Cycle snapshot exposes item/attempt/spending/feedback state → operator revises commitments/budget or reconciles outcome → stored state changes → reserve/runWork obeys the returned limits and accepted dependencies. - Boundary reachability: Long-term work desktop and trusted Host APIs are first-party shipped controls. WorkManager.trusted rejects administration/acceptance from active agent sessions. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: A supervisor controls one work item&#39;s execution, not the declared whole-cycle budgets/criteria. Removing operator choices leaves fixed admission rules, not autonomous whole-cycle regulation. - Whole-system current view: Cycle snapshot combines items, attempts, feedback, spend, reservations and unknown coverage at one directory/cycle. - Current-control decision scope: Whole-cycle budget, commitments/criteria, acceptance, continuation and reconciliation; P is a complete supported operator mode. - Evidence: docs/work-management.md; apps/desktop/src/host/work.ts lines 155–205, 340–438 and 538–650; apps/desktop/src/host/product-services.ts runWork. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: No whole-cycle S3=A is inferred from single-item supervisor delegation or hard concurrency/budget gates.\">P</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: No material first-party function established at the declared recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Independent challenge to routine operational correctness/acceptance claims. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No function-specific decisive-return path supplied. - Decision owner: No qualifying owner; operational agent choices, caller settings and runtime enforcement remain distinct. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Trusted user/validator feedback is the routine production acceptance path. Memory reviews receive journal snapshots without tools; registered candidate evaluators test proposed resource updates rather than supply a separately governed sporadic operational audit. - Closure path: Positive, function-specific constructor and eligible parent-mode alternatives checked; no qualifying closure remains. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The bounded absence concerns the organizational relation, not implementation purity. - Evidence: apps/desktop/src/host/memory-review.ts; docs/learning-resources.md - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high within the assessed distribution. - Caveats: A custom deployment could add the function; that is outside this shipped boundary.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:4\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: A(P) - Function: Adapt future execution capability/selection to observed external harness/model/provider task fit and corrected user requirements. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Different provider/model/effort suitability, reliability/cost coverage and task conditions make an old selection policy or skill unsuitable for later work. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Infer qualified future-use scenarios or prompt/skill changes from original execution and independent feedback, publish the chosen option into reusable selection knowledge; operator approval owns adoption in the alternate mode. - Decision owner: Base A: configured restricted Memory-review Agent chooses the adaptation content and Host applies validated plans. Parent P: authenticated local operator approves/rejects each staged option when writeApproval is enabled. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Frozen original-event snapshots, evidence validation, durable plans, revision checks, optional fixed resource evaluator, pending-write UI and next-run preparation. - Closure path: External/native behavior and user correction → bounded reviewer judges task/provider/effort fit and proposes future selection/skill option → automatic validated publication or operator decision → later swarm.prepare loads that evaluated concept and current feedback into operational selection. - Boundary reachability: ProductServices constructs AgentMemory with reviewMemory and resumes it after native runtimes attach. Default Memory settings enable autoReview; the packaged delegate skill requires current private combination evaluations. Optional approval mode is wired through pending changes and decide/applyReview. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Credit is limited to explicit scenario-sensitive future combination/skill adaptation, not storage or retrospective summary alone. Removing reviewer judgment leaves snapshots/revision gates but no chosen future suitability recommendation. Parent adoption is separately owned when enabled. - External distinction: Observed external provider/harness/model/effort behavior, user feedback and cost/reliability conditions, retained with scoped versions/coverage rather than fabricated universal rankings. - Future / prospective distinction: The delegate skill asks which scenarios should choose a different combination next time; learned options persist across tasks and new preparations, rather than only repair the current answer. - Adaptation option generated: Evidence-backed agent-selection concepts/Playbooks and registered prompt/skill candidates with counterevidence and limitations. - Path back into current capability / S3: selection() reloads reviewed concepts plus current corrected feedback into swarm.prepare; operator-owned cycle grants/budgets bound adoption and subsequent work. Registered file changes apply on native next load. - Evidence: packages/core/swarm/skills/delegate/SKILL.md sections Choose a combination / Improve the evaluations; apps/desktop/src/host/memory.ts selection(), review prompt and applyReview; apps/desktop/src/host/product-services.ts AgentMemory construction and callSwarm prepare; apps/desktop/src/memory.ts; docs/learning-resources.md. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high for supported selection-adaptation closure. - Caveats: No measured gain or general provider-quality claim. Only external/task-fit prospective adaptation is credited; ordinary memory writes and all generic self-improvement are not automatically S4. Learning disabled/grant-free deployments do not exercise this path.\">A(P)</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: No material first-party function established at the declared recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Identity/ultimate-policy disputes at Host-cycle recursion. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No function-specific decisive-return path supplied. - Decision owner: No qualifying owner; operational agent choices, caller settings and runtime enforcement remain distinct. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Permissions, user notes, standing criteria and policy settings constrain work. updatePolicy requires idle execution and stores grants, but no identity-level dispute-to-legitimate-parent-and-return process is supplied. - Closure path: Positive, function-specific constructor and eligible parent-mode alternatives checked; no qualifying closure remains. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The bounded absence concerns the organizational relation, not implementation purity. - Evidence: apps/desktop/src/host/product-services.ts updatePolicy; docs/permissions.md - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high within the assessed distribution. - Caveats: A custom deployment could add the function; that is outside this shipped boundary.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/e7f818d544b60c90b5684be978e78e0d34275ae9/assessments/sw/swarmx.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit e7f818d544b60c90b5684be978e78e0d34275ae9\">e7f818d &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-09-25T21:45:12+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/genai-io/san\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">San</a><small>2024</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-09-25\">2026-09-25</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/genai-io/san/tree/dd1b41882472fb76ce38dc3f88107deffc2e73bc\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision dd1b41882472fb76ce38dc3f88107deffc2e73bc\">dd1b418 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: transform user software-engineering/terminal objectives into repository, filesystem, command, research or other tool-mediated outcomes through a repeated reason→act→observe loop. - Disturbance / variety regulated: changing repository/environment state, ambiguous task requirements, tool output/errors, model responses, permission outcomes, context pressure and user feedback. - Decisive decision or feedback right: select substantive next model/tool actions and revise subsequent work from returned observations. - Decision owner: the active San main agent model. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: core run loop, tool registry, permissions/reviewer, session/transcript persistence, context management, provider adapters, skills/plugins/MCP and UI/runtime lifecycle. - Closure path: user objective enters session → model reasons and selects action/tool → San executes under policy → observation/result returns into conversation → model chooses next action from changed evidence → task/environment state changes and the cycle continues. - Boundary reachability: this is San&#39;s ordinary first-party interactive operating path, not a test-only or downstream composition. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: removing the model leaves execution/policy machinery but removes the substantive choice of what work to do next and how to react to tool evidence. - Evidence: internal/core/run.go; internal/agent/build.go; internal/agent/session.go. - Basis: explicit + structural -\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: no material first-party S2 relation is established at the assessed session recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: no qualifying inter-S1 interference/oscillation witness is established. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established. - Decision owner: none established for S2 at this boundary. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: subagent charters, bounded child execution, task/background machinery, session boundaries and concurrency protections can isolate/delegate work but do not by themselves establish coordination. - Closure path: not applicable; no reviewed path reconstructs distinct credited S1 units → concrete inter-S1 disturbance → S2-specific attenuation → feedback changing subsequent S1 behaviour. - Distinct S1 units: the long-lived main session loop is the established S1. One-shot subagents are bounded delegated workers and are not credited as separate viable S1s at the parent recursion merely from process/session plurality. - Inter-S1 disturbance: not established. - Attenuating coordination relation: not established beyond generic delegation/isolation/execution boundaries. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: not established for an S2-specific relation. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: it is deliberately not mapped as S2; the reviewed evidence supplies delegation and worker isolation, not the Profile&#39;s complete inter-S1 disturbance/attenuation witness. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: no S2 function is established, so ownership classification does not proceed. - Evidence: internal/subagent/executor.go; docs/concepts/harness-channels.md. - Basis: explicit + structural absence review -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: regulate the current San session&#39;s mission-level commitment and continuation between operational turns, deciding whether present work should continue autonomously, terminate as accomplished, or return to human authority. - Disturbance / variety regulated: locally completed turns that leave the mission incomplete, step-limit/truncation stops, recoverable failures, uncertain next steps, completed missions and situations that genuinely require a human-only decision/access grant. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose continue, done, or hand-back and, for continue, author the concrete next instruction sent back to the main agent. - Decision owner: the separate AutoPilot LLM judge/copot actor when AutoPilot mode is engaged. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: persisted AutoPilot mission/config, recent-transcript/evidence renderer, continuation/recovery budgets, stop-reason gates, UI submit path and deterministic validation of structured decision states. - Closure path: main S1 turn ends or stops resumably → AutoPilot receives mission plus recent whole-session evidence → AutoPilot chooses continue/done/hand-back → on continue its instruction is submitted as the next visible input to the main agent; on done the mission is retired; otherwise control returns to the user → subsequent current operation changes accordingly. - Boundary reachability: AutoPilot is a first-party session mode wired through the San app and can be started/configured from the product rather than requiring downstream code. - Whole-system current view: the controller receives the mission and bounded recent session evidence including conversation, compact summaries and tool outcomes, and is explicitly instructed to judge what is already accomplished from all supplied evidence rather than only the last turn. - Current-control decision scope: the session&#39;s current commitment to unattended mission execution—continue with a new concrete step, stop as complete, or escalate control back to the human. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: budgets/stop gates enforce safe boundaries, but they do not choose the mission-level continuation response or author the next instruction. Removing the AutoPilot model leaves those gates but removes the discretionary current-control judgment. - Evidence: internal/app/autopilot.go; internal/reviewer/reviewer.go. - Basis: explicit + structural -\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: no material first-party complementary-audit closure is established at the assessed session recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: San exposes operational transcripts and replay state that could reveal mismatches, but no first-party independent audit judgment/control loop is established over those observations. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established. - Decision owner: none established for S3 at this boundary. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: append-only transcripts, integrity/digest-aware replay, localhost read-only inspector, records/live-tail/state APIs and a permission reviewer. - Closure path: not applicable; the reviewed inspector path exposes operational reality but does not itself produce an independent corrective verdict that returns into runtime control. The permission reviewer acts before protected actions rather than auditing operational claims after/beside routine reporting. - Claim being audited: no qualifying first-party audit claim/loop established. - Ordinary reporting path: normal session messages/tool results/transcript records. - Complementary access path: inspector/replay can reconstruct raw transcript/replay state independently of the rendered assistant narrative. - Independence boundary: access is read-only and separate from the work loop, but access independence alone is insufficient without an audit judgment and feedback path. - Who acts on findings: not established as a first-party S3 closure; a human can inspect/debug, but generic human observation is not enough for the Methodology&#39;s autonomous audit state. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: no autonomous audit actor owns a complementary judgment. The pre-action permission reviewer owns a different function and is not reclassified as S3. - Evidence: internal/inspector/server.go; internal/inspector/replay.go; docs/packages/2-feature/inspector.md; internal/reviewer/reviewer.go. - Basis: explicit + structural absence review - Confidence: high - Caveats: the inspector is a strong constructor/debugging surface from which an auditor could be composed, but Methodology 0.3.6 does not award C until the S3 function itself is established.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no material first-party outside-and-then organizational adaptation loop is established at the assessed session recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: not established as an external/prospective adaptation function. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established. - Decision owner: none established for S4 at this boundary. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: self-learning can be triggered by the main model via Evolve; after a clean turn a restricted background fork reviews the completed conversation and can write durable project memory or agent-created skills that affect future sessions. - Closure path: not applicable for S4. The reviewed self-learning path closes retrospective experience consolidation into future memory/skills, but no first-party path reconstructs external/future-relevant sensing → development of adaptation options → S4 adaptation judgment → return into present capability/S3. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the main model autonomously decides when a turn is worth learning from and the review fork can decide what lesson/skill to write, but autonomy plus learning is not sufficient. The Profile explicitly excludes generic learning/self-improvement unless it participates in an external-and-prospective adaptation loop. - Evidence: docs/packages/2-feature/selflearn.md; internal/selflearn/reviewer.go; internal/selflearn/prompts.go. - Basis: explicit + structural absence review -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no material first-party identity/ultimate-policy decision loop is established at the assessed session recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: not established as an identity/ultimate-policy matter. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established. - Decision owner: none established for S5 within the standard session boundary. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: system-prompt identity/persona, rules, project/user memory, permission modes, AutoPilot mission/system prompt, reviewer decisions, skills and human approval constrain/configure operation but do not establish ultimate-policy closure. - Closure path: not applicable; no reviewed first-party path shows an identity/ultimate-policy issue reaching legitimate ultimate authority and returning as authoritative policy governing subsequent San operation. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: model actors operate inside configured identity, mission and permission boundaries; they are not shown exercising ultimate authority over those boundaries. Ordinary human permission or configuration edits likewise do not become S5 without an identity-level issue/decision/return loop. - Evidence: docs/concepts/harness-channels.md; internal/app/autopilot.go; internal/reviewer/reviewer.go. - Basis: explicit + structural absence review - Confidence: high - Caveats: San deliberately exposes configurable identity/policy channels; Methodology 0.3.6 does not treat their existence as S5 without the identity/ultimate-policy function itself.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/e4b2025677c98a7731c1bc46d6883f507f09c05f/assessments/san.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit e4b2025677c98a7731c1bc46d6883f507f09c05f\">e4b2025 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-09-17T19:15:26+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/aaif-goose/goose\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Goose</a><small>2024</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-09-15\">2026-09-15</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/aaif-goose/goose/tree/50666ae0b9a51e260b52b7efbab2e4e020346e94\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 50666ae0b9a51e260b52b7efbab2e4e020346e94\">50666ae &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: the standard runtime autonomously selects and executes actions/tools within configured bounds and iterates from results.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: no first-party S2 witness was established. The orchestrator permits top-level cross-session messages and permits delegated subagents to message siblings with the same parent, but generic communication does not identify a specific inter-S1 interference, conflict or oscillation together with a relation that attenuates it and feeds the result back into later S1 behavior. Subagent/fork support remains delegation rather than sufficient S2 evidence.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: the first-party orchestrator mode gives the autonomous top-level Goose agent a whole-system current view across sessions (listsessions / viewsession) and actual intervention authority over current commitments and operational capacity (startagent, sendmessage, interruptagent). These tools are directly model-visible and their effects are closed by the runtime; once the supported mode is enabled, a developer does not need to compose a separate autonomous actor, S3 decision authority, or closure loop. defaultenabled: false and hidden: true do not convert an otherwise closed agent-owned mode into C. The decisive evidence was already present at the accepted review ref, making this a same-ref correction rather than a new-ref architectural change.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: no alternative, sufficiently independent complementary audit path with materially different access to operational reality was established. Session viewing is part of the same S3 current-control surface, not a distinct audit relation.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: context management, retries and task reasoning concern current execution; no distinct externally and prospectively oriented intelligence loop that develops adaptation options and returns them into current capability was found.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: system prompts, permissions, extensions and runtime configuration remain developer/user supplied rather than an autonomous legitimate identity/ultimate-policy decision path.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/c18ecd44bdd2a63ce0893a114d25f5d3cc75a76a/assessments/goose.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit c18ecd44bdd2a63ce0893a114d25f5d3cc75a76a\">c18ecd4 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-09-19T18:08:39+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/Onelevenvy/flock\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Flock</a><small>2024</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-09-19\">2026-09-19</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/Onelevenvy/flock/tree/2eac827aa803ae2ee8578e401a864e116cd994d3\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 2eac827aa803ae2ee8578e401a864e116cd994d3\">2eac827 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: autonomously perform environment-facing task work through an iterative model/tool loop and return resulting observations into subsequent action selection. - Disturbance / variety regulated: heterogeneous user requests, changing local/project state, model responses, tool outputs/errors, sandbox/browser/process state and other execution observations requiring context-sensitive next actions. - Decisive decision or feedback right: select the next tool/action from current messages and tool results, then decide whether another operational action is needed or the task/run can terminate. - Decision owner: the active first-party Flock agent loop, including agent nodes used inside declarative workflows. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: AgentEngine::run, Flock&#39;s modelrequest/calltools graph, tool registry/executor, sandbox creation, checkpoint state, retries/timeouts, iteration limits and workflow state transport. - Closure path: task/messages/environment state → agent model selects a tool/action → first-party Flock tool/runtime executes it → tool result or changed environment returns into agent messages/state → agent chooses the next action or terminates. - Boundary reachability: this is the core shipped flock-agent path used by ordinary runs; workflow agent nodes expose the same class of bounded autonomous tool loop in the supported declarative workflow surface. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: deterministic graph/tool machinery transports and enforces execution, but it does not choose the context-sensitive next operational action; removing the model-driven agent leaves no owner of that S1 discretion. - Evidence: crates/flock-agent/src/engine/run/run.rs, crates/flock-agent/src/graph/builder.rs, crates/flock-workflow/src/nodes/agent.rs. - Basis: structural -\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: no material first-party S2 coordination path is established at the reviewed recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: not established; workflows can contain multiple operational agent nodes, but the reviewed shipped mechanisms do not identify a specific actual or structurally evidenced interference, conflict or oscillation among those S1 units that a coordination relation is designed to attenuate. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no S2-specific actor is shown choosing or revising a coordination response to cross-S1 disturbance. - Decision owner: none established for S2. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: graph edges, conditional routing, fan-out to multiple successors, shared workflow state, checkpointing, retries/timeouts and scheduler execution. - Closure path: these mechanisms move and sequence state/work among nodes, but no interference → attenuation decision → changed subsequent S1 behavior closure is evidenced. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: individual agent nodes own their local operational choices; graph topology and scheduler semantics are deterministic/user-authored. Neither fact establishes autonomous coordination of a concrete inter-S1 disturbance. - Evidence: crates/flock-workflow/src/builder.rs, crates/flock-workflow/src/nodes/agent.rs, crates/flock-agent/src/graph/mod.rs. - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: no whole-system current-control function is established in the reviewed standard distribution. - Disturbance / variety regulated: workflow progress, node failures, retries, timeouts and branch routing are regulated mechanically, but no first-party actor is shown regulating current shared resources, commitments, priorities or constraints on behalf of the whole Flock organization. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no shipped actor has both a whole-system current view and discretionary authority to revise relevant organization-wide commitments/resources/priorities/interventions. - Decision owner: none established for S3. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: workflow scheduler/compiled graph, checkpoints, retries/timeouts, cancellation state, workflow event sink, cron/headless execution controls and per-session approval state. - Closure path: these mechanisms close execution and error-handling transitions according to configured topology/policy, not a discretionary whole-system S3 control conversation. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: agent nodes select local task actions, while the workflow compiler/scheduler enforces configured structure. Neither worker selection nor deterministic current-state execution supplies the broader S3 decision right. - Evidence: crates/flock-workflow/src/builder.rs, crates/flock-workflow/src/nodes/common.rs, crates/flock-agent/src/engine/run/run.rs. - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: no material complementary operational-audit path is established for the current S1/S3 control boundary. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Flock exposes tool results, workflow events, retries and evaluation utilities, but no reviewed supported path independently challenges an operational unit&#39;s ordinary current-control claim and returns that finding into S3. - Decisive decision or feedback right: none established for S3 at the current-control layer. - Decision owner: none established for S3. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: workflow/tool event emission, retries, error state, checkpoints, logs, and grader/comparator/analyzer machinery bundled specifically inside the skill-development evaluation workflow. - Closure path: routine tool/workflow results feed the same operational path; skill-development graders/comparators feed S4 capability adaptation. Neither supplies a complementary current-operations audit channel returning findings to an S3 owner. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: independent graders can judge skill-development trials, but the audited claim there is prospective adaptation quality rather than current S1 operational accountability; moving that mechanism across functions would be a category error. - Evidence: crates/flock-workflow/src/nodes/agent.rs, flock-data/skills/skill-creator/SKILL.md. - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:4\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: A - Function: develop and test reusable future Flock capability in response to capability needs and externally/future-relevant information, then return the resulting adaptation into the skill surface used by later operation. - Disturbance / variety regulated: a user&#39;s desired capability may not exist or an existing skill may perform poorly; relevant documentation, similar skills, best practices, edge cases, model/tool behavior, evaluation failures and changing triggering behavior create future-facing distinctions that should change the reusable capability. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose the concrete skill design and revisions—workflow/instructions/resources, evaluation cases/assertions, changes suggested by trial transcripts/benchmarks, reusable helper scripts and triggering-description changes—then decide whether further adaptation iterations are warranted within the user&#39;s stated objective/feedback. - Decision owner: the active first-party Flock agent following the shipped skill-creator adaptation procedure. The user/Parent supplies the capability goal, may confirm intent/evals and can stop or accept the loop, but the agent develops, tests and revises the adaptation options rather than merely persisting a user-authored artifact. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: packaged skill-creator instructions/resources; research through available tools/MCP where useful; with-skill versus baseline trial runs; graders/comparators/analyzer; benchmark aggregation; human feedback; description-optimization loop; filesystem skill format; skill loader/discovery and packaged application skill resources. - External distinction: relevant external documentation, similar skills, best practices, available tool/model behavior and user-environment requirements can expose capability gaps or better approaches that are not contained in the current skill alone. - Future / prospective distinction: the loop asks whether a reusable skill will handle representative future tasks and trigger appropriately, comparing candidate capability against baselines/previous versions and held-out or expanded evaluations rather than merely repairing the current turn. - Adaptation option generated: a new or revised reusable SKILL.md plus supporting scripts/resources, evaluation design and triggering description selected by the agent from those distinctions and trial results. - Path back into current capability / S3: the selected skill is written or packaged into Flock-readable user/project/workspace/install skill locations; flock-skills subsequently discovers/loads it into later agent contexts, changing the capability available to present operational runs. - Closure path: capability need + relevant external/future distinctions → agent researches/designs a reusable skill option → with-skill/baseline trials and feedback expose performance distinctions → agent revises the skill/description/resources → resulting skill is written/packaged in a Flock-readable skill location → later agent runs load/use the changed capability. - Boundary reachability: skill-creator is under flock-data/skills, those resources are included in the Tauri application bundle, and flock-skills resolves the installed skills resource directory plus user/project skill directories. This is a shipped product capability, not repository-only CI/dogfood. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: user intent and acceptance constrain the adaptation, but the substantive future-capability option generation, diagnosis of trial failures, revision choices and reusable artifact construction are delegated to the agent. Deterministic loaders/eval scripts support and measure the loop rather than owning those adaptation decisions. - Evidence: flock-data/skills/skill-creator/SKILL.md, crates/flock-skills/src/paths.rs, crates/flock-skills/src/discovery.rs, flock-ui/src-tauri/tauri.conf.json. - Basis: structural - Confidence: medium-high - Caveats: generic loading/discovery of already-authored skills alone would not establish S4. Credit depends on the separately shipped adaptation procedure that actively researches/develops/tests/revises a capability and closes it back into the runtime-readable skill surface. Some evaluation branches depend on optional subagent/CLI facilities; the core draft → test/review → revise → persist loop remains explicitly adapted for environments without those facilities.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no identity- or ultimate-policy-level closure is established at the reviewed Flock recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: ordinary tool risk and human intervention are regulated, but no evidenced organizational identity/policy issue is routed to a legitimate ultimate authority whose decision returns as non-routine global policy for subsequent operation. - Decisive decision or feedback right: none established for S5. - Decision owner: none established for S5 at this boundary. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: ToolApprovalManager, per-session Default/AutoEdit/Yolo modes, per-category “always” approval, sensitive-tool approval, human nodes/ask-human/request-human-assistance, sandbox permissions and workflow configuration. - Closure path: human/tool approvals can approve or deny an ordinary pending action and session modes can change which tool categories auto-approve, but that is task/session authorization rather than identity/ultimate-policy closure balancing current operations and future adaptation. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: approval machinery enforces user/session choices; neither it nor an agent is shown owning legitimate ultimate organizational identity/policy authority. Ordinary human final say over a tool call does not meet S5 by itself. - Evidence: crates/flock-core/src/ipcinterface/approval.rs, crates/flock-workflow/src/nodes/agent.rs, crates/flock-tools/src/tools/builtin/askhuman.rs, crates/flock-tools/src/tools/sandbox/requesthumanassistance.rs. - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/c8624b55eaa098004a856ac116b7881495866f8f/assessments/flock.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit c8624b55eaa098004a856ac116b7881495866f8f\">c8624b5 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-09-21T17:11:20Z\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/xerrors/Yuxi\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Yuxi</a><small>2024</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-09-21\">2026-09-21</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/xerrors/Yuxi/tree/86c67dc41249c3c9cad9e976fd8a006194d39a76\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 86c67dc41249c3c9cad9e976fd8a006194d39a76\">86c67dc &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: perform user-directed knowledge, analysis and artifact-producing work through a first-party model/tool execution loop. - Disturbance / variety regulated: heterogeneous user requests, retrieved knowledge, files/workspace state, tool/API results, sandbox execution outcomes, model/tool errors and changing conversation/task context. - Decisive decision or feedback right: select substantive next actions/tools and decide when available operational evidence is sufficient to continue, delegate or return a result. - Decision owner: the running Yuxi model agent. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Yuxi ChatbotAgent, configured runtime tools, composite filesystem/sandbox backend, checkpointer, request/run worker lifecycle, retries, context summarization, Skills/MCP and knowledge integration. - Closure path: persisted request → prepared Yuxi agent graph → model-selected tool/action → first-party execution → ToolMessage/observation → subsequent model decision → final persisted result. - Boundary reachability: the standard Yuxi API/worker path directly instantiates and executes ChatbotAgent; no downstream custom harness is required to obtain the model/tool operational loop. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: LangGraph executes graph mechanics and external providers supply model inference, but the model actor owns the discretionary operational choices while Yuxi owns the executable loop, tools/context and durable run path. - Evidence: ARCHITECTURE.md, backend/package/yuxi/agents/base.py, backend/package/yuxi/agents/buildin/chatbot/graph.py. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: generic LangGraph/DeepAgents capabilities not assembled and exposed by Yuxi are not inherited.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: no material first-party path establishes attenuation of a concrete interference, conflict or oscillation among at least two distinct Yuxi S1 units. - Disturbance / variety regulated: not established as S2 at the reviewed recursion. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established for S2. - Decision owner: not established for S2. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: child AgentRun lifecycle, same-thread no-concurrent-write rule, FIFO request queues, run leases, shared runtime/workdir, parent cancellation and delegation exist but are not credited as coordination by topology or serialization alone. - Closure path: not applicable. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the root can manage child commitments, but evidence inspected does not identify a concrete cross-child disturbance and an S2-specific attenuation relation whose feedback changes subsequent child behavior. - Evidence: ARCHITECTURE.md, backend/package/yuxi/agents/middlewares/subagenttask.py. - Basis: structural absence review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: shared workspaces can create potential interaction, but potential collision is not evidence that Yuxi ships a concrete S2 attenuation loop.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: regulate the current portfolio of subordinate Yuxi agent commitments during a root-agent run. - Disturbance / variety regulated: incomplete, delayed, failed, unnecessary or still-running subordinate tasks; changing need for specialist work as child results arrive; risk of finalizing while required delegated work remains unresolved. - Decisive decision or feedback right: decide what child work to start, inspect current child status/progress, await results when needed, cancel a child, continue root work in parallel and condition subsequent commitments/finalization on current child state and returned results. - Decision owner: the root Yuxi model agent. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Yuxi SubAgent middleware; durable parent/child AgentRun relation; subagentstart, subagentstatus, subagentcancel, subagentawait; ChatBotState.subagentruns; child ownership verification; cancellation/request services; persistent run/result state. - Closure path: root identifies current need → starts one or more configured subordinate operations → child run state/result returns as ToolMessage plus subagentruns state → root inspects/awaits/cancels/launches further work as current conditions require → subsequent root action/final answer changes accordingly; remaining live children are cancelled when parent ends. - Boundary reachability: standard configured ChatbotAgent construction automatically installs the SubAgent middleware whenever visible/configured Yuxi subagents exist; the root receives the current-control tools directly in its normal model loop. - Whole-system current view: the root graph state accumulates structured summaries for each child run keyed by runid, including current status, agent identity, timing/error/artifact/result fields; explicit status/await calls refresh individual commitments before the root acts. - Current-control decision scope: start new subordinate commitments, continue prior child threads when idle, inspect their progress/results, wait for required completion, cancel no-longer-wanted work and decide whether the root can safely finalize. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: repositories/services enforce ownership and durable state, but the root model decides which current commitments exist and how their live evidence changes ongoing portfolio action. Removing the model leaves lifecycle mechanics but no discretionary whole-run allocation/control decision. - Evidence: backend/package/yuxi/agents/buildin/chatbot/graph.py, backend/package/yuxi/agents/buildin/chatbot/state.py, backend/package/yuxi/agents/middlewares/subagenttask.py, ARCHITECTURE.md. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: this mapping is conditional on the standard supported mode with configured/visible SubAgents; a single-agent configuration still retains S1 but does not instantiate a multi-unit portfolio.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: no material first-party path establishes a sufficiently independent complementary challenge to operational reality with corrective return into S1/S3. - Disturbance / variety regulated: not established as S3. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established. - Decision owner: not established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: model/tool audit events, Langfuse integration, run/debug timelines, knowledge/RAG evaluation, human tool approval, retries and child status/results all exist but serve ordinary execution, evaluation or parent governance. - Closure path: not applicable. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: child outputs are production inputs to the root, not a structurally independent audit channel; dashboard/evaluation evidence does not by itself exercise corrective operational authority; human approval is Parent intervention rather than S3. - Evidence: ARCHITECTURE.md, backend/package/yuxi/agents/toolapproval.py, backend/package/yuxi/agents/middlewares/subagenttask.py. - Basis: structural absence review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: a separately configured reviewer subagent could be constructed downstream, but role naming or constructibility is not a shipped independent audit closure.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no material first-party path establishes autonomous or parent-governed prospective environment-facing adaptation of Yuxi organizational capability. - Disturbance / variety regulated: not established as S4. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established for prospective capability adaptation. - Decision owner: not established for S4. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: knowledge/RAG retrieval, persistent checkpoints, user Memory, dynamically activated Skills, configurable models/tools/MCP and reusable agent/subagent definitions broaden available capability but do not themselves decide and integrate a future organizational adaptation from environmental distinctions. - Closure path: not applicable. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the memory middleware explicitly writes long-term memory only when the user asks it to remember; Skills are selected/configured resources and dynamic activation occurs within the present run. Persistence/reuse is not prospective S4 closure. - Evidence: README.md, backend/package/yuxi/agents/middlewares/memory.py, backend/package/yuxi/agents/middlewares/skills.py. - Basis: structural absence review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: administrators/users can change knowledge, agent, Skill, MCP and model configuration, but generic external configuration does not establish Parent-governed S4 without a qualifying S4 issue/option/decision/return path.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no material first-party path establishes identity- or ultimate-policy-level adjudication that closes unresolved present/future tension for the Yuxi organization. - Disturbance / variety regulated: not established as S5. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established for identity/ultimate policy. - Decision owner: not established for S5. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: multi-tenant RBAC, administrator configuration, agent prompts/descriptions, tool approval mode, model/resource visibility and human approvals constrain operation. - Closure path: not applicable. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: these are access/configuration and parent safety controls. The reviewed repository does not establish a function-specific identity/ultimate-policy issue and an authority that adjudicates it and returns the decision into operation. - Evidence: README.md, ARCHITECTURE.md, backend/package/yuxi/agents/toolapproval.py. - Basis: structural absence review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: super-admin/operator authority is real product governance but does not become VSM S5 without the identity/ultimate-policy organizational function.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/f0e739507a67e4069c7ef24add9a14d2bfcea7ec/assessments/yuxi.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit f0e739507a67e4069c7ef24add9a14d2bfcea7ec\">f0e7395 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-22T22:58:40+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/AutoCodeRoverSG/auto-code-rover\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">AutoCodeRover</a><small>2024</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-22\">2026-10-22</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/AutoCodeRoverSG/auto-code-rover/tree/585d3e639aeda58ef0b6a151dd1cc2721a94d267\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 585d3e639aeda58ef0b6a151dd1cc2721a94d267\">585d3e6 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: investigate a supplied code issue and produce an applicable candidate patch through autonomous search and code-edit choices. - Disturbance / variety regulated: uncertainty about code locations, incompatible search requests, non-applicable diffs, failed reproduction and regression outcomes. - Decisive decision or feedback right: select iterative search APIs and suspected bug locations, generate patch content, and revise attempts after code/tool feedback. - Decision owner: first-party search/patch model interactions; operator selects provider and issue, while deterministic backends execute code searches and validation. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: SearchBackend AST-aware calls, API-call proxy, diff extraction/applicability, optional SBFL and test execution, bounded retries and candidate selection. - Closure path: issue + repository → model-selected code search → returned source context → model-generated patch → applicability/test/reviewer feedback → next patch or output selection. - Boundary reachability: the main source-search/patch path is the default github-issue, local-issue and SWE-bench work mode and does not require the experimental reviewer. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: model-generated search instructions and patch content vary with observed task data; configured round ceilings, tool execution and user-provided issue are supporting restrictions, not owners of those decisions. - Evidence: search selects APIs and consumes outcomes, model-generated applicable patch and feedback, pipeline. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high for the shipped model/tool/patch control loop; no claim about the patch&#39;s external task success. - Caveats: live model-backed operation was not tested; ordinary validation is opt-in and some tasks fall back to patch-only generation.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: A - Function: in the experimental reproducer/reviewer mode, dampen conflict between independently proposed issue reproducer and corrective patch by adjudicating which of the two must be revised. - Disturbance / variety regulated: a patch can make a faulty test pass, or a valid patch can seem wrong because the test incorrectly represents the issue; indiscriminate iteration on only one cell would perpetuate mismatch. - Distinct S1 units: TestAgent autonomously proposes executable reproducing tests; PatchAgent autonomously proposes code changes, each with a dedicated model prompt, artifacts and feedback/revision history. - Inter-S1 disturbance: their joint candidate outcome disagrees with the observed buggy/patched reproducer results and issue semantics, with the conflict potentially attributable to either test construction or patch construction. - Attenuating coordination relation: an independent reviewer model is given the issue, both artifacts and original/patched execution evidence and returns separate patch-correct / test-correct judgments and targeted advice. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: ReviewManager.generator routes patchdecision == NO to PatchAgent.addfeedback and writeapplicablepatchwithfeedback, while testdecision == NO updates TestAgent.addfeedback and writereproducingtestwithfeedback; the resulting artifacts are re-executed on the following round. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: the reviewer is explicitly asked to settle which of two distinct operational outputs is defective, and that decision changes which S1 producer receives a corrective instruction. Merely executing tests or selecting the last patch is not the credited S2 relation. - Decisive decision or feedback right: dynamically judge whether to correct the test, patch or both from cross-cell evidence; the Python manager routes the judgment. - Decision owner: first-party model reviewer in the opt-in mode, not the fixed CLI scheduler or a user-assembled external supervisor. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: original/patched repro execution, two-sided review JSON, separate feedback histories, bounded review rounds and validation. - Closure path: test + patch candidates → execute both interpretations → reviewer locates mismatch → affected producer receives focused feedback → regenerated test/patch enters next comparison. - Boundary reachability: the first-party SWE-bench --reproduce-and-review switch installs this loop when a valid reproducer exists; it is experimental, off by default, and not exposed for general local/GitHub issue commands. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the correctness allocation and advice are discretionary reviewer-model output; the manager&#39;s branches only implement the model&#39;s two judgments. This does not imply shared-system scheduling, generic writer-conflict mediation or whole-current governance. - Evidence: flag and scope, mode gate, two-sided reviewer prompt, feedback dispatched to each agent, reproducer feedback memory. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium; the narrowly defined test/patch coordination closure is explicit, but no model-run has established effectiveness or a broader autonomous S2. - Caveats: opt-in experimental SWE-bench only; no reproducer means fallback to patch-only generation. The review loop is quality-focused, does not coordinate parallel workers or resource contention, and its S2 interpretation depends on treating the test and patch generators as distinct operational units rather than one monolithic coding S1.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: direct present resource, commitment, priority and accountability trade-offs of multiple operating units for the whole assessed harness. - Disturbance / variety regulated: model/task retries, concurrent batch workloads, failing patch attempts and tool execution time. - Decisive decision or feedback right: none established at an S3-specific organizational level; task grouping, fixed retry limits and patch selection are preset process management. - Decision owner: human CLI/config author and deterministic orchestration, with model decisions local to repair tasks. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: ProcessPoolExecutor, group sequencing, overall retry limit, model cycling, validation and generated patch selection. - Closure path: user-supplied task groups → parallel/serial processing → per-issue output; no separate current-wide negotiation/reprioritization and corrective allocation among an accountable whole. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: process limits and ranking candidate patches do not own whole-organization resource/commitment decisions. - Evidence: task group scheduling, bounded attempts and selection. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high for the bounded first-party negative. - Caveats: a separate operator organization can add current management, but is outside this assessed runtime.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: independently challenge an S3 current-management claim using a complementary route to operational reality, then return findings for corrective control. - Disturbance / variety regulated: potentially false patch-correctness claims and misleading local tests. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no such S3 audit right; the experimental agentreviewer decides whether local code/test artifacts are correct as ordinary repair-loop QA. - Decision owner: model reviewer and deterministic validator under S1/S2 repair production, not a distinct management auditor. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: test execution against original/patched code, reviewer feedback JSON, optional regression validation and logs. - Closure path: code/test pair → reviewer/validator → revise task artifacts, not S3 claims → independent operational audit → S3 corrective management return. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the reviewer is model-owned but its object is the code patch and reproducer, not complementary verification of whole-current S3 management. - Evidence: reviewer&#39;s actual task, manager feedback scope, local patch validation. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high for task-level QA versus S3 boundary. - Caveats: the term &quot;reviewer&quot; alone does not establish audit ownership at organizational recursion.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: monitor wider future environment, propose adaptive changes and return selected options to current harness capability. - Disturbance / variety regulated: unfamiliar future issue classes, external library changes, provider variation and changing codebases. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no evidenced prospective organizational capability decision; model selection, fault localization and retrieval are for the present issue. - Decision owner: user/developers choose providers, install new methods and maintain the project; the repair model chooses present-task actions. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: model alternatives, AST search, issue context, regression feedback, retries and an external paper/benchmark. - Closure path: issue/code evidence → patch for the same issue, or configured provider fallback; not future trend sensing → strategic adaptation → returned capability change. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: solving a previously unseen issue is S1 operational adaptation, not an installed outside-and-then intelligence function. - Evidence: pipeline and model retry, public research and scope statement. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high for reviewed runtime. - Caveats: external researchers can measure and change future versions, but their decisions are outside the one-run harness boundary.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: settle ultimate purpose and binding identity/policy conflicts for the running organization. - Disturbance / variety regulated: requested edits outside intended scope, provider-policy rejection, task-local constraints and permissible execution. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no first-party ultimate policy/identity decision path; user provides issues/models/options and model produces candidate patches subject to configured guards. - Decision owner: human operator/developer for values and configuration, with external provider policy enforced by provider. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: CLI tasks, prompt instructions, errors/exception fallback and test validation. - Closure path: user-supplied goal/config → issue repair attempt; no identity-level disagreement → legitimate ultimate authority → binding policy decision returned to the operating system. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: prompt-level do/don&#39;t rules and fallback on an external content-policy exception do not transfer supreme policy authority to the coding agent. - Evidence: CLI goal and model selection, provider-policy exception fallback, patch prompt scope. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high for bounded negative at the local runtime. - Caveats: broader project governance and legal restrictions are external arrangements.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/3be8d80335ca7cbb047936f15ea0705c6d94f3dd/assessments/au/autocoderover.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 3be8d80335ca7cbb047936f15ea0705c6d94f3dd\">3be8d80 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-09-26T23:41:42+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/OS-Copilot/OS-Copilot\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">OS-Copilot / FRIDAY</a><small>2024</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-09-26\">2026-09-26</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/OS-Copilot/OS-Copilot/tree/f720af8807e49a92dda64572d2c6bc6c0ac7ee7e\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision f720af8807e49a92dda64572d2c6bc6c0ac7ee7e\">f720af8 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: autonomously plan and execute concrete computer/OS/application work needed to satisfy a user task, adapting task-local actions to returned execution state. - Disturbance / variety regulated: heterogeneous user goals, available tools, filesystem/application state, generated-code failures, missing intermediate operations, execution outputs/errors and changing prerequisites within a task. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose/decompose the next substantive computer actions, generate or select executable tools, interpret returned execution state and decide whether to continue, amend or replan the active task. - Decision owner: first-party model-driven FRIDAY planner/executor/judge path. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: tool retrieval, dependency graph/topological ordering, Python/Shell/AppleScript/API execution environments, bounded repair iterations, task state, generated-tool repository and deterministic loop/control plumbing. - Closure path: user task -&gt; model decomposes/selects actions -&gt; generated/retrieved tool executes against OS/application environment -&gt; result/error/filesystem state returns -&gt; model judges/replans/repairs -&gt; changed action executes -&gt; task state updates. - Boundary reachability: quickstart.py and documented examples instantiate FridayAgent, while FridayAgent.run() directly invokes the credited planner/executor/refinement path. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: deterministic code transports state and executes selected code, while substantive task decomposition, tool generation, result judgment, amendment and replanning choices are model-driven. - Evidence: README.md; oscopilot/agents/fridayagent.py; oscopilot/modules/planner/fridayplanner.py; oscopilot/modules/executor/fridayexecutor.py. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: the README notes single-round conversation, but the internal execution of one task is multi-step and result-conditioned; conversational turn count does not break the operational loop.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: no material first-party S2 path regulating a concrete disturbance between distinct autonomous S1 units is established at the declared FRIDAY-installation recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: the planner orders dependencies among subtasks and modules exchange artifacts, but those subtasks/modules are components of one operational agent rather than separately accountable S1 units producing an inter-operation oscillation/interference problem. - Decisive decision or feedback right: none established for inter-S1 coordination. - Decision owner: none established for S2. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: dependency graph, topological ordering, prerequisite-result passing, shared tool repository and sequential subtask loop. - Closure path: task decomposition creates ordered work inside S1, but no distinct S1-A/S1-B interaction disturbance -&gt; coordinating judgment -&gt; changed later behavior of those S1 units loop closes. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: sequencing dependencies is operational planning, not evidence of the VSM S2 organizational function. - Evidence: oscopilot/agents/fridayagent.py; oscopilot/modules/planner/fridayplanner.py. - Basis: structural negative search. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: a deployment containing several separately accountable FRIDAY instances could create an S2 problem, but that is a different system-in-focus.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: no distinct first-party whole-system current-control function over multiple or separately managed operational units is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: task-local execution failures, weak outputs and missing prerequisites are regulated, but they are handled inside the same S1 episode that performs the work. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no separate actor is evidenced with authority to form a whole-installation current view and intervene in the present operational organization beyond the active task-local plan/repair loop. - Decision owner: none established for S3; FRIDAY&#39;s planner/judge retains task-local S1 action authority. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: in-band judgetool, repair loop, replanning, task graph/status, score threshold and bounded repair count. - Closure path: execution state -&gt; same operational agent judges/repairs/replans -&gt; same task continues; this closes S1 recovery, not a separate management loop over the current whole. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: model-driven replanning is real discretion, but its organizational function is local task execution/recovery rather than whole-system current control. - Evidence: oscopilot/agents/fridayagent.py; oscopilot/modules/executor/fridayexecutor.py; oscopilot/modules/planner/fridayplanner.py. - Basis: structural negative search. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: names such as planner or orchestrating agent are not promoted to S3 without a whole-system current-view/current-control witness.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: no first-party independent complementary-audit channel with distinct access/judgment and corrective return is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: FRIDAY evaluates generated code/output and may repair or replan, but this is ordinary in-band operational verification. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no independent auditor is evidenced with a separate claim-checking path over operational reality. - Decision owner: none established for S3; judgetool uses the ordinary executor module/model configuration inside the same task loop. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: LLM judgment prompt, execution result/error/filesystem observations, score threshold and repair/replan path. - Closure path: operation -&gt; same in-band executor/judge evaluates -&gt; same agent repairs/replans -&gt; operation continues; no independent complementary path returns findings to a separate corrective owner. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: semantic judging exists, but independence/complementary access required for S3 is absent. - Evidence: oscopilot/modules/executor/fridayexecutor.py; oscopilot/modules/basemodule.py; oscopilot/agents/fridayagent.py. - Basis: explicit + structural negative search. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: calling the component a judge or critic does not satisfy S3; no separate reviewer/model/provider/evidence channel comparable to an independent audit path is configured by the standard runtime.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:4\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: A - Function: generate and implement prospective reusable computer capabilities from external software/package/demo context so later operational tasks can perform work the current repertoire could not. - Disturbance / variety regulated: capability gaps exposed by unfamiliar software/application domains, new packages/APIs and task classes not covered by the existing generated-tool repository. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose a prospective course/lesson set for the selected external software/package context and, through lesson execution, generate reusable tools that expand the future action repertoire. - Decision owner: first-party SelfLearner model for course design plus the model-driven FRIDAY lesson execution path that produces qualifying reusable tools. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: software/package/demo inputs, prior-course history, model-authored course JSON, SelfLearning.learncourse, ordinary FRIDAY task execution, score threshold, persistent generated-tool files/JSON/Chroma vector database and retriever similarity search. - Closure path: operator identifies a capability domain -&gt; learner reads software/package/demo/prior-course context -&gt; model designs prospective lessons -&gt; FRIDAY executes lessons -&gt; successful generated Python capabilities are persisted -&gt; later ordinary task queries the same repository -&gt; newly learned tool descriptions/code are retrieved and influence execution -&gt; previously unsupported task becomes executable. - Boundary reachability: courselearning.py is a shipped first-party entrypoint documented by the self-learning tutorial; no downstream code changes or custom orchestration are required to create and reuse the adapted capability. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the operator selects the broad learning target, while the first-party model determines the course/lessons and FRIDAY generates/qualifies the executable tools; deterministic repository code only persists and retrieves those chosen capabilities. - External distinction: software/package context and optional demo-file contents describe capabilities outside FRIDAY&#39;s existing action repertoire; the documented example uses the external openpyxl library and an Excel file. - Future / prospective distinction: the learning mode is explicitly run to acquire tools for later tasks, rather than only repairing the presently failing task; its persisted output survives the learning episode and is queried in subsequent task planning/execution. - Adaptation option generated: a model-authored course of new capability lessons and the reusable generated tools produced by executing those lessons. - Path back into current capability / S3: qualifying tools are written into generatedtools.json, code/description files and the persistent vector index; ordinary FridayRetriever then automatically retrieves them for later S1 task planning/code generation, changing the executable repertoire without repository-source modification. - Evidence: courselearning.py; oscopilot/agents/selflearning.py; oscopilot/modules/learner/selflearner.py; oscopilot/modules/executor/fridayexecutor.py; oscopilot/toolrepository/manager/toolmanager.py; oscopilot/modules/retriever/vectorretriever.py; docs/source/tutorials/selflearning.rst. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: self-learning is a separate supported operating mode rather than automatically triggered from every failed task. That affects invocation, not ownership of the adaptation option once the mode is invoked. The broad learning domain/package remains operator-supplied and is not treated as autonomous S5 policy.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no first-party runtime identity/ultimate-policy adjudication loop is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: user task, model/provider settings, score/repair limits, available tools and self-learning domain inputs constrain behavior, but they are task/configuration/capability controls rather than organizational identity or ultimate-policy decisions. - Decisive decision or feedback right: none established for defining/reconciling FRIDAY&#39;s identity, ultimate purpose or legitimate highest-level policy under competing operational/adaptation demands. - Decision owner: none established for S5. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: environment/configuration, prompts, thresholds, user task/learning inputs and static runtime structure. - Closure path: configuration/task inputs directly constrain S1/S4 behavior; no identity-level issue -&gt; legitimate ultimate authority -&gt; authoritative policy decision -&gt; returned governance loop is evidenced. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: FRIDAY exercises operational and capability-adaptation discretion, but not authority to redefine its own organizational identity or ultimate policy. - Evidence: README.md; oscopilot/modules/basemodule.py; oscopilot/agents/fridayagent.py; courselearning.py. - Basis: structural negative search. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: user-supplied objectives/learning targets are parent inputs, but ordinary task/capability target selection is not S5=P without an identity/ultimate-policy function.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/df12b45707a2cb0c30902dcd8b2df91a67f603c7/assessments/os-copilot.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit df12b45707a2cb0c30902dcd8b2df91a67f603c7\">df12b45 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-12T14:55:12+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/agentscope-ai/agentscope\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">AgentScope 2.0</a><small>2024</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-12\">2026-10-12</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/agentscope-ai/agentscope/tree/5ff52f877de12d66a30d55af279dd4f42b1590f3\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 5ff52f877de12d66a30d55af279dd4f42b1590f3\">5ff52f8 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Configurable first-party model-directed reasoning/acting tool loop performs user/worker tasks and returns tool/environment feedback. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Unknown task conditions, tool execution responses/errors, context limits and external model results. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Choose goal-specific tool calls, perform actions, interpret outcomes and continue or finish. - Decision owner: AgentScope-configured agent model in the SDK/Service&#39;s direct agent execution mode. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Model adapters, Toolkit, middleware hooks, workspace/sandbox, permission gates, message/session store. - Closure path: User or leader prompt → agent reasoning → selected tool action → tool/environment result → model continuation and final worker report. - Boundary reachability: The published SDK and example Agent Service initialize real AgentScope Agent instances with toolkit/model and feed messages into replystream (README.md; src/agentscope/agent/agent.py; src/agentscope/app/service/chat.py). - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The model selects substantive task acts; host/session middleware only applies technical limits and transport policy. - Evidence: src/agentscope/agent/agent.py; src/agentscope/app/service/chat.py; src/agentscope/tool/toolkit.py. - Basis: structural -\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-unknown\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: ? - Function: Candidate team message routing and session locking, but no proven function-specific first-party inter-S1 interference attenuation with returned feedback. - Distinct S1 units: Separately instantiated model-driven members of a first-party TeamRecord, each with its own session/task and message bus. - Inter-S1 disturbance: Potential conflicting simultaneous operations against shared task resources or overlapping workspace targets; the service&#39;s mere presence of separate sessions does not establish the specific disturbance regulation. - Attenuating coordination relation: The observed session lock serializes calls within one session; TeamSay routes messages and avoids misdelivery, but a function-specific inter-member contention/coordination policy has not been demonstrated. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: Team messages and leader reassignments exist; their connection to mitigation of a particular verified inter-worker conflict remains unproven. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: No positive S2 attribution is made from generic team topology, messaging, per-session locks or leader assignment alone. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Candidate inter-member resource contention, not established as an implemented control target. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Unresolved for interference-specific arbitration among independent worker sessions. - Decision owner: Potential leader or host service, not yet reconstructable for a material S2 relation. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: MessageBus, TeamSay, sessionrun lock, team membership constraints, toolkit role filtering. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: No positive S2 owner is credited; the leader sends messages and the service serializes individual session turns, but neither path is proven to own inter-worker interference-specific decisions. - Closure path: Inter-agent messages are delivered; no defensible concrete collision → arbitration → modified workers path established. - Evidence: src/agentscope/app/tool/teamsay.py; src/agentscope/app/service/chat.py; src/agentscope/app/tool/teamcreate.py; src/agentscope/app/service/toolkit.py. - Basis: unknown -\">?</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: Model-led team leader makes current team staffing, delegation, repair and follow-up decisions based on worker reports. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Pending delegated tasks, changed worker outcomes, failures, information gaps and incomplete task progress. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Create a team, select role/worker, assign task, request clarifications and continue work after returned findings. - Decision owner: First-party hosted model leader using TeamCreate/AgentCreate/TeamSay, not a deterministic background task dispatcher. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: TeamRecord, role-separated team tools, agent/worker session allocation, message bus, report-enforcing middleware. - Whole-system current view: Leader receives team roster and completion/failure reports of the team it created; no claim of full multi-tenant service-wide control. - Current-control decision scope: Assign current work, change team tasks, re-instruct workers and integrate returned work in the bounded team recursion. - Closure path: Worker performs task and sends mandatory TeamSay report → leader model receives result → chooses follow-up or new task → worker session/action changes. - Boundary reachability: The Agent Service toolkit grants leader-specific team tools in configured leader mode; worker reports are actively enforced by middleware and persisted/routed back to the leader (src/agentscope/app/service/toolkit.py; src/agentscope/app/tool/agentcreate.py; src/agentscope/app/middleware/teammembermiddleware.py). - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The actual choices of child role, scope and continuation are model-generated via shipped team tools; technical message delivery and individual session run locks are merely enforcement. - Evidence: src/agentscope/app/tool/teamcreate.py; src/agentscope/app/tool/agentcreate.py; src/agentscope/app/tool/teamsay.py; src/agentscope/app/middleware/teammembermiddleware.py. - Basis: structural -\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-unknown\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: ? - Function: GoalPipeline provides separate executor/verifier agents and revision feedback, but complete independent operational-artifact challenge path is not established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: False executor goal-completion statements, missing required deliverables. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Verifier returns pass/fail about executor goal and an explanation, which may prompt another executor iteration. - Decision owner: Separately configured verifier Agent, subject to its actual tools/evidence access. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: GoalPipeline executor/verifier alternation, structured execution report, structured verification result. - Claim being audited: Executor&#39;s report that it fulfilled the original goal. - Ordinary reporting path: Executor returns a structured report under ExecutionReport. - Complementary access path: The verifier receives original goal plus executor&#39;s text report; supported independently sampled source/tool artifact inspection is not guaranteed by this pipeline contract. - Independence boundary: Distinct Agent object is possible, but materially independent observation from primary operational artifacts is unresolved. - Who acts on findings: Pipeline sends failure feedback back to executor, conditional on the verifier&#39;s structured judgment. - Closure path: Report → verifier model check → pass/fail → continue or return; missing independent observational basis prevents positive state. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: An independent model call is necessary but not sufficient when its only guaranteed input is the producer&#39;s own claim. - Evidence: src/agentscope/pipeline/goalpipeline.py; src/agentscope/app/middleware/teammembermiddleware.py. - Basis: unknown -\">?</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-unknown\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: ? - Function: Persistent memory, scheduling and middleware extension may support reuse across future tasks but do not yet establish an outside-and-then capability adaptation decision. - Disturbance / variety regulated: New task conditions, changing knowledge and external user contexts. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No confirmed owner/loop deciding a prospective adaptation option to future team capability. - Decision owner: Unresolved between user, configured agent, memory backend and developer. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Long-term memory middleware, scheduler manager and goal/planning toolkit. - External distinction: External observations may be retained in memory, but this alone is not systemic intelligence. - Future / prospective distinction: Reuse is future-facing in time but not necessarily prospective organizational adaptation. - Adaptation option generated: Not established from the read-only source evidence. - Path back into current capability / S3: Memory/context or schedule modifications can affect future runs; full future intelligence → change is unproven. - Closure path: Captured memory → later prompt is technically supported, but no established S4 choice/feedback. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Memory retention and schedules are mechanisms, not proof of autonomous or parent S4. - Evidence: src/agentscope/middleware/longtermmemory; src/agentscope/app/manager/scheduler/schedulermanager.py; src/agentscope/tool/task/updatetask.py. - Basis: unknown -\">?</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-unknown\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: ? - Function: Team identities, access/permission rules and human confirmations exist without established ultimate-policy identity decision/return loop at the bounded team recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Permissions over tools, resource access, multi-tenant rights and delegation. - Decisive decision or feedback right: The human/application owner sets permission rules and may approve actions, but ultimate organizational identity/policy closure is unresolved. - Decision owner: User/app role policies; not automatically a first-party agent policy controller or a qualified parent S5. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: PermissionEngine, TeamRecord roles, workspace permissions and HITL middleware. - Identity / ultimate-policy issue: Allowed identity, roles and acceptable operating permissions of configured team members. - Ultimate authority in each claimed mode: No S5-specific autonomous/parent owner established; execution-level rules are narrower than ultimate policy. - Return-to-operation path: Permission decisions guard specific tool calls and inheritance to child agents, not a proven changing-policy feedback loop. - Closure path: Permission request/policy → allow/deny action, lacking ultimate identity-level decision. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: A policy engine enforces rules but does not decide the system&#39;s ultimate purpose or identity. - Evidence: src/agentscope/permission/engine.py; src/agentscope/app/tool/agentcreate.py; src/agentscope/app/service/toolkit.py. - Basis: unknown -\">?</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/44d6c86f16e424c65ea333f05cf07553822f7db5/assessments/agentscope-2-0.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 44d6c86f16e424c65ea333f05cf07553822f7db5\">44d6c86 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-09-16T22:21:05Z\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/letta-ai/letta\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Letta</a><small>2023</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-09-15\">2026-09-15</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/letta-ai/letta/tree/5bcdd177d70fa2b31a754cfcd801e77b2e1ab16a\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 5bcdd177d70fa2b31a754cfcd801e77b2e1ab16a\">5bcdd17 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: the primary agent owns model/tool action decisions and can delegate bounded work to first-party subagents.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: subagent launch, parallel execution and lifecycle tracking do not establish an autonomous anti-oscillation/shared-resource coordination decision right among peer S1 units.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: the subagent manager schedules/delegates tasks but does not own whole-system current priorities, resources and commitments as a superior regulator.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:3\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: the default compaction-triggered reflection path launches a distinct autonomous background reviewer over already-completed primary-agent conversations. It searches for mistakes, contradictions and durable corrections and has an automatic integration path into parent memory/skills. This is a separate complementary audit/correction loop rather than ordinary self-reflection inside the S1 turn.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: reflection is retrospective internal learning from the agent&#39;s own conversations. It does not establish a distinct outside-and-then environmental intelligence function coupled to strategic adaptation.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: reflection may maintain memory/persona/skills, but ultimate identity, policy and authority remain constrained by parent/runtime configuration; editable memory is not legitimate S5 closure.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/ae8749aa005ea42801ed86e32476f5c417ecd9a0/assessments/letta.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit ae8749aa005ea42801ed86e32476f5c417ecd9a0\">ae8749a &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-09-16T23:24:44+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/FoundationAgents/MetaGPT\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">MetaGPT</a><small>2023</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-09-15\">2026-09-15</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/FoundationAgents/MetaGPT/tree/11cdf466d042aece04fc6cfd13b28e1a70341b1f\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 11cdf466d042aece04fc6cfd13b28e1a70341b1f\">11cdf46 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: specialized role agents autonomously produce requirements, designs, code, analyses and other operational artifacts.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: the reviewed leader/SOP path primarily assigns and sequences work. No separate evidence shows a function that dampens destructive interference or oscillation among autonomous S1 units without centralizing their decisions.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: the default TeamLeader has a whole-team current view through team information, routed feedback and a structured plan; it can regulate current commitments by assigning specialists, closing tasks, changing/resetting the plan and gating downstream work on prerequisite results. This goes beyond worker selection alone and is exercised by an autonomous shipped role. Parent-authored roles, SOPs, prompts, budget and team composition constrain this loop but do not provide a separate first-party human/operator decision path over the same current whole-team commitments, so no (P) mode is published.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: review/testing inside the normal software-production path does not provide a materially independent alternative access channel to operational reality.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: planning, product/competitive artifacts and replanning are project work; the reviewed runtime does not establish a distinct external-and-prospective adaptation loop in two-way conversation with S3.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: role definitions, SOP, prompts, budget and team composition are parent/developer authored. No runtime agent holds legitimate ultimate identity/policy closure for the system-in-focus.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/9ee1ea7472777767928c8b2ab035e0948a0467e9/assessments/metagpt.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 9ee1ea7472777767928c8b2ab035e0948a0467e9\">9ee1ea7 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-09-18T23:12:56+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/lobehub/lobehub\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">LobeHub</a><small>2023</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-09-18\">2026-09-18</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/lobehub/lobehub/tree/3af256caae11796da471bec5f57d882853f30f19\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 3af256caae11796da471bec5f57d882853f30f19\">3af256c &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: specialized agents autonomously perform assigned conversational, research, creation or tool-using work. - Disturbance / variety regulated: open-ended user tasks, domain-specific information, tool/model observations and task-specific uncertainty. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose the substantive actions and output needed for the agent&#39;s assigned task or conversational role. - Decision owner: the invoked model-driven agent. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: agent configuration, system role, model/provider selection, plugins, isolated async task context and session state. - Closure path: agent receives an instruction, performs work, returns a result to the group/user, and that result becomes input to subsequent orchestration or conversation. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the model-driven agent owns substantive task execution while LobeHub transports context/tools/results. - Evidence: README.md, docs/usage/agent/agent-team.mdx, agent-management manifest/system role. - Basis: explicit + structural -\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: A - Function: coordinate multiple specialized S1 agents by choosing dependent versus parallel work, agent selection, handoff order and group interaction mode. - Disturbance / variety regulated: dependency-sensitive work that would be wrong in parallel, redundant/overlapping contributions, poor handoffs and unstructured multi-agent interaction. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose whether to use one agent, broadcast, sequential speaking, one async task or multiple parallel tasks, and choose the participating agents/instructions. - Decision owner: the model-driven Group Supervisor / Orchestrator. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: speak, broadcast, executeAgentTask, executeAgentTasks, group orchestration runtime, shared group context and supervisor resume after member completion. - Closure path: supervisor analyzes the request, selects a coordination pattern and dispatches agents; member results return to the supervisor, which synthesizes, follows up or changes the next interaction. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: deterministic runtime executes the selected pattern, but the supervisor model owns the coordination discretion over mode, agent selection and follow-up. - Evidence: packages/builtin-tool-group-management/src/systemRole.ts, docs/usage/agent/agent-team.mdx, group orchestration runtime. - Basis: explicit + structural - Confidence: high - Caveats: generic callAgent alone would not establish S2; the positive mapping depends on the explicit dependency/parallelism decision framework and returned supervisor loop. - Distinct S1 units: two or more specialized agents in an Agent Group, each capable of separate operational work. - Inter-S1 disturbance: dependent subtasks can be run in the wrong order, overlapping agents can add redundant work, and independent subtasks can be unnecessarily serialized; first-party guidance also calls out weak handoffs and overlapping roles as collaboration failures. - Attenuating coordination relation: the Group Supervisor chooses sequential, parallel, broadcast or focused modes and assigns work to agents according to dependency and expertise. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: member outputs resume the supervisor; it can ask follow-ups, synthesize, invoke another agent or terminate the group turn, thereby changing later contributions. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: first-party supervisor policy explicitly diagnoses dependence/parallelizability and selects an attenuation pattern for interactions among distinct operational agents, with returned member results driving further coordination.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: no separate whole-group current-control function over resources, commitments, priorities or constraints is established beyond the S2 orchestration itself. - Disturbance / variety regulated: group task routing, model choice and current conversation state were inspected, but no distinct S3 current-control loop is established. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no material first-party owner was found with a whole-system current view and separate authority over group-wide commitments/resources/accountability. - Decision owner: none established for qualifying S3. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: agent management, group profile editing, async task execution, scheduling and supervisor orchestration. - Closure path: no qualifying distinct S3 loop was found. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the orchestrator&#39;s evidenced rights are inter-agent task/mode coordination for the current group operation; naming it supervisor/operator is not enough to promote that same function to S3. - Evidence: group-management system role, Agent Group docs, agent-management tools. - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: C - Function: review another agent&#39;s produced work through a distinct reviewer/editor role and return critique into revision/re-review. - Disturbance / variety regulated: incomplete, unclear, inconsistent or low-quality outputs that require a second perspective before acceptance. - Decisive decision or feedback right: judge produced work, issue feedback and require/refine another contribution. - Decision owner: constructor path; LobeHub supplies explicit iterative/reviewer group patterns, but the developer/user must compose the reviewer role, independence and authority in the concrete group. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: iterative Agent Group mode, distinct agent identities, sequential handoffs, shared context and supervisor-mediated repeated turns. - Closure path: producer emits work; reviewer/editor returns critique; producer revises; reviewer can perform final review in the documented iterative pattern. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: a separate model-driven reviewer can own the judgment once composed, but the standard distribution does not assign an invariant independent reviewer or audit authority automatically. - Evidence: docs/usage/agent/agent-team.mdx, packages/builtin-tool-group-management/src/systemRole.ts. - Basis: explicit - Confidence: medium - Caveats: ordinary debate or synthesis is not counted as S3; the positive constructor claim is limited to the explicitly documented review/refine pattern with distinct roles. - Claim being audited: that another agent&#39;s draft/output is sufficiently correct, clear or complete for the group&#39;s purpose. - Ordinary reporting path: producer agent returns its draft/result through the normal group turn. - Complementary access path: a separately configured Editor/reviewer/QA agent receives the produced work and can critique it in an iterative group workflow. - Independence boundary: first-party group mechanics preserve distinct agent identities, but organizational independence/authority of the reviewer is supplied by the group composition rather than guaranteed by runtime policy. - Who acts on findings: the producing agent receives reviewer feedback and revises; the group/supervisor can route the revised result back for final review.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no separate external/prospective organizational intelligence loop is established at the Agent Group boundary. - Disturbance / variety regulated: user preference/history changes are learned, but no distinct future/environment adaptation function is closed. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no material first-party owner was found that senses external/future distinctions, generates adaptation options and returns a selected change into group capability/S3. - Decision owner: none established for qualifying S4. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Personal Memory, editable structured memory, continual learning and tool/search capability. - Closure path: memory changes later agent context directly; no separate outside-and-then option-development loop is established. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: personalization/learning and external tools do not automatically establish S4. - Evidence: README.md, Agent Group/runtime docs. - Basis: explicit + structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no identity/ultimate-policy governance function is established for the Agent Group. - Disturbance / variety regulated: group prompt, member roster and agent configuration can change, but no ultimate identity dispute/authority loop is evidenced. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no material first-party identity-level authoritative decision with a return-to-operation closure was found. - Decision owner: none established for qualifying S5. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: group profile, Agent Builder, agent CRUD, prompts, skills and user configuration. - Closure path: configuration edits alter operation, but they are not an evidenced S5 identity/ultimate-policy process. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: prompts and editable configuration are implementation/control artifacts, not proof of VSM S5. - Evidence: docs/usage/agent/agent-team.mdx, agent-management manifest/system role. - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/9f83e41179680c24cbe23a5091abda11fcc221d0/assessments/lobehub.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 9f83e41179680c24cbe23a5091abda11fcc221d0\">9f83e41 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-12T16:19:31+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/kyegomez/swarms\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Swarms</a><small>2023</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-12\">2026-10-12</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/kyegomez/swarms/tree/3009364a7e44dc3e55fec238b56b3a4fa6949a05\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 3009364a7e44dc3e55fec238b56b3a4fa6949a05\">3009364 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Model-directed individual first-party Agent solves a task via model/tool choice, observation and iterative continuation. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Dynamic goals, changing tool outcomes, external model output, context and task errors. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Choose steps, call tools/subagents and decide when to continue or finish an operating task. - Decision owner: Per-agent hosted LLM/Agent runtime, not merely an agent-shaped tuple invoked by a deterministic sequencer. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Agent run, runautonomousloop, tool executor, model wrapper and workspace. - Closure path: Task → model-generated action/tool use → environment result → next model reasoning and eventual completion. - Boundary reachability: Python Agent.run and optional autonomous-loop configuration are used from the published distribution (README.md; swarms/structs/agent.py). - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Context-sensitive model actions select operating acts; loop counters and provider wrappers do not own that choice. - Evidence: swarms/structs/agent.py; README.md. - Basis: structural -\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: C - Function: In bounded GroupChat mode, mediate multiple model-agent bids competing for a single shared conversational speaking slot, preventing speaker domination while retaining useful contributions. - Distinct S1 units: A configured GroupChat.agents set of independently invoked Agent objects, each producing a context-contingent (score, reply) via model-backed respond tool. - Inter-S1 disturbance: Several operating agents seek the same shared conversation floor simultaneously; unchecked publication produces competing turns, and repeated high-frequency speakers dominate the shared information channel. - Attenuating coordination relation: collectbids calls each agent, selectspeaker applies a shared threshold and recency penalty, chooses exactly one speaker, and post updates common conversation history seen by all peers on the next turn. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: The selected speaker&#39;s contribution, not all conflicting bids, enters shared history for every agent&#39;s following model turn; recent speakers are penalized in future selections. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: The explicit gate is driven by simultaneous claims to a scarce common conversational medium and recurring dominance, with a fairness attenuation policy and returned altered information environment. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Conflicting simultaneous bids, message crowding and disproportionate conversational control. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Select exactly one admissible turn among competing agent contributions. - Decision owner: First-party deterministic GroupChat floor arbiter, therefore C, not a separate agentic S2 decision owner. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: respond(score,message) tool, threshold, recency window/penalty, chosen-speaker posting and shared Conversation. - Closure path: Independently formed bids → contention/fairness gate → one allowed published contribution → next turn&#39;s changed conversation and adjusted future bids. - Boundary reachability: GroupChat.run uses the packaged bidding, selecting and posting operations; this is an opt-in published architecture, not an inactive helper (swarms/structs/groupchat.py; README.md). - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Agents determine proposed content and scores; the decisive inter-agent floor grant is deterministic host policy (C). - Evidence: swarms/structs/groupchat.py; swarms/structs/conversation.py; README.md. - Basis: structural - Confidence: medium - Caveats: Applies to GroupChat&#39;s shared conversation medium, not arbitrary shared file changes or all Swarms topologies. Does not claim broad resource conflict arbitration.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: Model-led HierarchicalSwarm director assigns present worker orders and adjusts ongoing team work from collected subordinate results. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Divergent worker progress/results, uncertain task division, errors and changing completion quality. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Produce a model-derived order batch, choose which specialist performs what, inspect returned outputs and issue fresh orders in further loops. - Decision owner: First-party configured model director; deterministic parseorders and executeorders relay its decisions. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: HierarchicalSwarm director, OrderBatch parser, worker dispatch and optional feedback director, conversation storage. - Whole-system current view: Director sees the bounded swarm conversation/worker results and prior execution history, not every organization using Swarms. - Current-control decision scope: Current worker allocation, ordering, revisions and reattempts for the HierarchicalSwarm instance. - Closure path: Director plans and orders → specialized workers run → results return and enter conversation → director next loop reassigns/replans. - Boundary reachability: HierarchicalSwarm.run calls step, rundirector, parseorders, executeorders, and loops over result feedback when maxloops allows (swarms/structs/hiearchicalswarm.py). - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Actual work orders originate in a model-led director within the shipped topology; loop counters and dispatcher enforce, not replace, task choices. - Evidence: swarms/structs/hiearchicalswarm.py; README.md. - Basis: structural -\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-unknown\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: ? - Function: Some prebuilt modes include judge/verification or consensus phases; materially independent operating evidence inspection and correction path remains unresolved. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Invalid subordinate claims, low-quality task completion or conflicting agent assertions. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Judge can evaluate outputs; whether it has independent primary-source evidence and authority over correction is not guaranteed. - Decision owner: Configured judge/consensus agent, not established as a truly separately observing auditor. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Hierarchical judge toggle, MajorityVoting consensus and HeavySwarm verification-oriented roles. - Claim being audited: Specialist worker answer/output claims. - Ordinary reporting path: Worker results added to director conversation, majority-voting conversation or final report. - Complementary access path: Shipped judging paths chiefly pass worker outputs/derived conversation text; a separate direct observation of work artifacts has not been established at the first-party boundary. - Independence boundary: A different model/persona is not a guaranteed independent observations channel. - Who acts on findings: Directors or consensus can summarize and reconsider, but an independent audit finding → corrective operational reroute is unproven. - Closure path: Output → judge/consensus verdict → report or next loop, insufficient to classify a distinct S3 audit under the governing method. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Another LLM rating peer output is not equivalent to a complementary function sampling reality independently. - Evidence: swarms/structs/hiearchicalswarm.py; swarms/structs/majorityvoting.py; swarms/structs/heavyswarm.py. - Basis: unknown -\">?</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-unknown\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: ? - Function: Planning prompts, persistent workspace and memory may help later tasks but a decision owner adapting future systemic capability from external prospective observations has not been established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Future requirements, market or environment shifts and changing task complexity. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Unresolved among user-supplied swarm config, model director and persisted task memory. - Decision owner: No uniquely identified first-party outside-and-then intelligence controller at the bounded swarm recursion. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Conversation/workspace storage, planning role prompts, dynamic agent layouts. - External distinction: Outside facts can enter model context, but information access alone is not S4. - Future / prospective distinction: Multi-loop plans address current task commitments, not shown to adapt future organization capability. - Adaptation option generated: No verified capability-evolution choice produced by a distinct owner. - Path back into current capability / S3: Persistence and plan prompts can affect later runs, but external foresight → applied organizational redesign remains unresolved. - Closure path: Retained context → later work, incomplete future-intelligence and capability-change loop. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Prompted planning and memory are not enough for S4. - Evidence: swarms/structs/hiearchicalswarm.py; swarms/utils/workspacemanager.py; swarms/structs/agent.py. - Basis: unknown -\">?</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-unknown\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: ? - Function: Configured swarm identity, roles and execution limits exist but an ultimate organizational purpose/policy closure is not traced. - Disturbance / variety regulated: User boundaries, acceptable agent/tool behavior and system-level authority. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Developer/user chooses prompts, members and parameters; no proven ultimate-policy choice by a qualified first-party actor. - Decision owner: User-supplied configuration for role identity and deterministic safety checks; a complete parent policy mode remains unresolved. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: system prompts, task role configuration and tool/platform restrictions. - Identity / ultimate-policy issue: Who chooses and revises the organization&#39;s overarching identity, purpose and final authority. - Ultimate authority in each claimed mode: Not established beyond out-of-band user/developer configuration. - Return-to-operation path: Prompt and parameter selection guides run behavior, but no complete governing policy feedback is proven. - Closure path: Configured goals/roles → worker execution; no verified policy challenge/ultimate revision returned to operations. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: A system prompt or fixed role roster is not itself an ultimate decision loop. - Evidence: swarms/structs/hiearchicalswarm.py; swarms/structs/groupchat.py; swarms/structs/agent.py. - Basis: unknown -\">?</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/5b1d5a9c5a355aedd4320e3898fd3b2af3f52800/assessments/swarms.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 5b1d5a9c5a355aedd4320e3898fd3b2af3f52800\">5b1d5a9 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-09-17T21:31:18+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/camel-ai/camel\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">CAMEL Workforce</a><small>2023</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-09-17\">2026-09-17</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/camel-ai/camel/tree/8c791b7b9cf7deab56cb5a92818c34499af9097f\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 8c791b7b9cf7deab56cb5a92818c34499af9097f\">8c791b7 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: A. Worker agents autonomously execute assigned task work through model/tool loops.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S2 / coordination: C. Workforce maintains explicit cross-task dependency state and gates release/order of work across multiple workers. This prevents dependent S1 work from proceeding against unsatisfied predecessor commitments, establishing a concrete coordination path beyond routing. The mechanism is supplied deterministically by Workforce while the application supplies the task graph, so ownership is constructor-side rather than agent-owned.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: A. The Coordinator operates over shared workforce state, assigns work based on worker capability, tracks current commitments, handles failures, and can create new workers or redirect/replan work. These are whole-workforce current-control rights over assignments and available operational capacity. The model-driven coordinator owns the runtime choice within configured bounds.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: —. Quality/failure analysis evaluates returned task results in the ordinary Workforce control/reporting path. No materially complementary evidence-access channel or protected independent audit boundary was established. The historical C therefore does not survive current S3 criteria.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: —. Replanning and dynamic worker creation respond to current-task failure; they do not establish an external/future environmental intelligence loop that changes organizational capability.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: —. Human pause/stop/skip controls and the externally supplied objective are parent/operator controls over ordinary execution, not a demonstrated ultimate-policy/identity closure path. The historical P is therefore removed.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/cfe27303e117e1fd3a79fc8f485f33caada591a4/assessments/camel-workforce.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit cfe27303e117e1fd3a79fc8f485f33caada591a4\">cfe2730 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-09-19T19:14:57+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/micro/go-micro\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Go Micro</a><small>2015</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-09-19\">2026-09-19</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/micro/go-micro/tree/792b18c3010715e2f12621f0120bf058be225482\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 792b18c3010715e2f12621f0120bf058be225482\">792b18c &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: autonomously transform user/task state through a model → tool/service/delegation → observation loop until an operational result is produced. - Disturbance / variety regulated: heterogeneous user goals, changing service/tool availability, tool/RPC/A2A results, persistent conversation state, provider responses, tool failures and intermediate observations requiring context-sensitive next actions. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose the next service/custom/built-in tool, decide whether to plan or delegate, consume returned observations, continue the loop or settle the answer. - Decision owner: the active Go Micro LLM agent in NewAgent or micro chat direct-service mode. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: model adapters, service registry/tool discovery, built-in plan/delegate/request-input tools, memory/store, tool wrappers, step/loop/spend limits, approvals, checkpointing, retry/timeout logic, RPC/A2A/MCP integration. - Closure path: user/assigned task + current context → agent selects action/tool/delegation → Go Micro executes/gates it → tool/service/sub-agent result returns into model context → agent selects another action or final result. - Boundary reachability: NewAgent and micro chat are shipped, documented first-party runtime modes; the positive S1 loop is implemented in agent/ and CLI runtime code rather than inferred from examples or repository dogfood. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: removing the model-driven agent while retaining registry, memory, tool execution and guardrails leaves no contextual actor choosing the next operational action. Deterministic wrappers constrain and transport the model&#39;s decisions rather than replacing them. - Evidence: README.md, agent/builtin.go, cmd/micro/chat/chat.go. - Basis: structural - Confidence: high - Caveats: external model inference and remote A2A peers are substrate/environment. The positive state is established independently by the first-party agent loop around those dependencies.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: no material first-party S2-specific loop was established that regulates a concrete interference/conflict/oscillation among distinct Go Micro S1 agent units at the declared product-runtime recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: candidate paths inspected included multiple registered agents, RPC/A2A handoffs, service discovery/load balancing, durable flows, duplicate delegate/retry protection and shared registry/store infrastructure. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no qualifying S2-specific decision right is supplied. Delegation selects another actor for a subtask; service discovery/load balancing routes calls; idempotency/retry controls duplicate side effects; flow edges order work. - Decision owner: none established for S2 in the reviewed standard product-runtime boundary. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: registry, client load balancing, broker/store, plan/delegate tools, A2A/MCP transport, flow sequencing, retry/idempotency guards and isolated ephemeral sub-agent context. - Closure path: no first-party path was found that detects a specific interaction disturbance among two distinct S1 agents, selects an attenuation response for that disturbance, and feeds the coordination result back into subsequent local S1 behaviour. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: multi-agent reachability, routing and delegation are present, but the Methodology explicitly requires more than communication/handoff. The reviewed product runtime does not supply an S2-specific autonomous or constructor path tied to a concrete inter-S1 oscillation/conflict witness. - Evidence: README.md, agent/builtin.go, gateway/a2a/a2a.go. - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: no distinct first-party whole-system current-control function was established beyond local agent planning/delegation and deterministic runtime enforcement. - Disturbance / variety regulated: candidate current-control paths inspected included store-backed plans, delegation, flow execution state, registry discovery, tool approvals, spend/step/loop limits and runtime stop/resume mechanisms. - Decisive decision or feedback right: the ordinary agent may revise its own task plan and delegate subtasks, but no separate product-runtime path was found with a whole-system current view plus authority to bargain/regulate shared resources, commitments or priorities on behalf of multiple operational units. - Decision owner: none established for S3 at the declared recursion. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: plan memory, delegate routing, flow state/checkpoints, registry/client machinery, tool/step/loop/spend limits, approvals and cancellation/timeouts. - Closure path: no S3-specific whole-system feedback/intervention loop was established. Local plan updates return to the same agent&#39;s task execution; deterministic limits enforce preselected constraints. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: a parent agent that decomposes one user request and delegates one subtask is not automatically S3. The reviewed evidence does not show a standard mode where an agent receives a whole-system operational view and owns ongoing resource/commitment intervention across independent S1s. - Evidence: agent/builtin.go, README.md, flow/. - Basis: structural - Confidence: high - Caveats: .github/loop has project-level planning/current-work behavior, but it belongs to the excluded repository-development organization and is not borrowed into the product-runtime S3 mapping.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: C - Function: challenge a flow-step result through a distinct verification judgment and return rejection feedback into another operational attempt before the flow proceeds with the bounded result. - Disturbance / variety regulated: plausible but deficient step outputs that ordinary body execution would otherwise return without a separate grade-and-correction pass. - Decisive decision or feedback right: the Grader decides pass/fail and supplies corrective feedback; Verify threads rejection feedback into the next body attempt and records the final verification outcome. - Decision owner: Go Micro does not ship a sufficiently independent default autonomous auditor for the general case. LLMGrader reuses the flow model and only sees the latest result. The application must compose a grader with independence appropriate to the audited claim; Go Micro supplies the S3-specific verification/feedback closure. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: bounded Verify attempt loop, feedback-field injection, optional backoff, verification metadata, flow checkpoint/result state and optional LLMGrader helper. - Closure path: body produces candidate result → composed grader audits it → rejection/feedback enters the next body input → body retries → accepted or bounded-failure result becomes the flow state. - Claim being audited: whether the current flow-step output satisfies the deployment-defined rubric/ground truth before it is accepted as the step&#39;s result. - Ordinary reporting path: the body step returns its State as normal flow output. - Complementary access path: the first-party Grader seam invokes a separately composed grading actor after body completion; the deployment can give that actor external ground truth, raw artifacts or a distinct model/service, and Go Micro closes its verdict back into retry. - Independence boundary: independence is not guaranteed by the built-in LLMGrader; it is intentionally left to the developer-supplied Grader. This is why the state is C, not A. - Who acts on findings: flow.Verify deterministically reinjects grader feedback into the next body attempt; the operational body/agent then produces the corrected candidate. - Boundary reachability: flow.Verify, the Grader contract and feedback/retry closure are shipped public first-party flow primitives at the reviewed revision, not CI-only tests. A deployment only needs to supply the independent grading actor/ground-truth access. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the function-specific audit path and return loop are first-party, but the decisive sufficiently independent audit actor is not supplied in a standard autonomous mode. General custom-code extensibility is not the basis; the credit is for the dedicated Verify/Grader primitive whose semantics are verification judgment plus corrective return. - Evidence: flow/verify.go, flow/verifytest.go. - Basis: explicit - Confidence: medium-high - Caveats: same-model LLMGrader alone is not treated as sufficient complementary independence. A deployment that wires only that helper may not operationally realize S3 even though the first-party constructor exists.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:4\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: A(P) - Function: detect that current operational capability cannot satisfy an external requirement, develop a service-level adaptation, materialize it, and return the new capability into subsequent agent operation. - Disturbance / variety regulated: new user needs not covered by existing registered services/tools; requests to extend or modify an existing system; design/code/compile failures encountered while materializing the capability. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Base mode — the micro chat agent decides that no existing service can handle the request and chooses microgenerateservice; the generation models choose the service architecture/handler changes. Parent mode — the user reviews the LLM-designed system proposal and decides at Generate? [Y/n] whether the adaptation should be materialized. - Decision owner: Base mode — the autonomous chat/generation model path. Parent mode — the human operator owns the decisive accept/reject adaptation judgment, while the generator autonomously develops and implements the approved option. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: registry/tool discovery, generate.Design, existing-service scan, deterministic service-structure generation, LLM handler generation, bounded compile-fix loop, process startup, registry wait and tool refresh. - Closure path: external capability request + current service inventory → missing-capability distinction → model designs service adaptation → autonomous choice or parent approval triggers generation → code is written/compile-fixed/built/started → registry/tool set refreshes → subsequent agent turns can use the new service. - External distinction: the user asks for a capability absent from the current registered service/tool environment, or asks to extend/modify the existing system. - Future / prospective distinction: Go Micro models a desired future system/service capability relative to the current inventory rather than only selecting among existing tools; Design explicitly receives existing services and constructs the complete extended target design. - Adaptation option generated: one or more new/modified Go Micro services with schema/endpoints, generated handler/business logic and an updated managing agent/tool surface. - Path back into current capability / S3: generated services are compiled and started, allowed to register, micro chat refreshes discovered tools, and the new endpoints become immediately usable by the same operational agent; in initial generation mode the approved system becomes the subsequent running service/agent environment. - Boundary reachability: both micro chat autonomous missing-capability generation and micro run --prompt / CLI user-approved generation are shipped documented CLI modes. The positive mapping does not depend on repository CI or .github/loop dogfood. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: in base mode removing the model&#39;s missing-capability judgment/service-design choices leaves only a generator with no contextual adaptation decision. In parent mode the human decides whether the proposed adaptation is admitted, and that decision is returned through the same generator into executable capability. - Evidence: cmd/micro/chat/chat.go, cmd/micro/cli/generate/generate.go, cmd/micro/run/run.go, README.md, flow/analyze.go. - Basis: explicit - Confidence: high - Caveats: the adaptation path is developer/runtime oriented rather than production hot-patching of an arbitrary deployed service; the credited mode is nevertheless first-party and explicitly returns generated service capability into the supported running micro chat environment. flow.Analyze/LLMOptimizer alone would be only a partial adaptation constructor because applying its prompt proposal is caller-owned.\">A(P)</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no product-runtime identity/ultimate-policy decision loop was established at the declared recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: candidate paths inspected included agent system prompts, auth/rules, tool approvals, spend/step/loop limits, requestinput, generated-service confirmation and repository North Star/maintainer restrictions. - Decisive decision or feedback right: none of the product-runtime mechanisms escalates a genuine identity/ultimate-policy issue to a legitimate ultimate authority and returns that decision to govern subsequent operation at this recursion. - Decision owner: none established for S5 in the assessed product runtime. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: auth rules/JWT wrappers, guardrails, tool approvals, static prompts/configuration, human input pauses, spend budgets and generation confirmation. - Closure path: ordinary task/action approvals and configuration changes can alter execution, but no identity/ultimate-policy issue → legitimate authority → authoritative decision → returned policy loop was found. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: policy text, permission checks and human approvals bound ordinary operations; they do not by themselves own system identity or ultimate policy. Repository NORTHSTAR and off-limits maintainer decisions belong to the excluded repository-development organization. - Evidence: agent/builtin.go, auth/, internal/docs/CONTINUOUSIMPROVEMENT.md. - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/9a9faa2e1208ecb2712c492398de7b7e7f24976a/assessments/go-micro.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 9a9faa2e1208ecb2712c492398de7b7e7f24976a\">9a9faa2 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr data-a=\"1\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-11T21:55:22+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/WittyRyuji/WittyCode\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">WittyCode</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"1\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>1</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-11\">2026-11-11</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/WittyRyuji/WittyCode/tree/67af7d8fb0851cd4623c7b94993deddcb766ec1f\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 67af7d8fb0851cd4623c7b94993deddcb766ec1f\">67af7d8 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: choose and carry out model-directed local coding and information actions for the user&#39;s request. - Disturbance / variety regulated: changing local repository contents, search output, command errors, user steering, interrupted runs, provider rate limits, context pressure and ambiguous next steps. - Decisive decision or feedback right: the model chooses tool name and arguments, interprets returned observations and decides to continue or finish within the first-party multi-step loop. - Decision owner: selected API/ChatGPT-backed model in the shipped WittyCode agent runtime; the user sets the permission envelope. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: ToolContext, command/file tool registry, approval/Plan restrictions, tool-output clipping, streaming adapters, retry and cancellation handling, persisted session log, compaction and step cap. - Closure path: user prompt → controller-created AgentLoop → model call → permitted local action → tool result appended as functioncalloutput → subsequent model choice or final response; /goal can add bounded further turns. - Boundary reachability: the README&#39;s packaged desktop first-party ChatGPT Plan/API Provider modes instantiate AgentLoop from the production controller; they do not require the external Codex or Devin CLI agent loop. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: action selection and iteration depend on model judgment over tool observations; permission checks and persistence enforce or transport that judgment rather than selecting the action. - Evidence: runtime loop and action return, production controller wiring, permissions, goal loop. - Basis: structural and explicit. - Confidence: high for first-party control wiring; medium for provider-side run behavior. - Caveats: source-level assessment, not a paid provider or Windows desktop E2E run; /goal uses a model self-verdict and fixed deterministic limits, not independent verification.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: no distinct first-party cross-S1 conflict-attenuation function is demonstrated at the selected one-session recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: tool calls, queued steering messages and permission requests within a single conversation may contend, but they do not constitute conflict between independent S1 operating units. - Decisive decision or feedback right: deterministic per-turn tool sequencing and session state transitions; no cross-unit coordination discretion is shown. - Decision owner: the single active model for its task, with deterministic controller sequencing; no separate S2 agent is present. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: serial runcalls, queued steering and session lifecycle state. - Closure path: one session&#39;s model calls are executed and observed in order; no distinct S1 collision is detected and returned as an inter-unit coordination adjustment. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: alternate model engines are choices for the same session, not simultaneously operating S1 units; sequential tool calls are within-S1 execution discipline. - Evidence: loop call execution, controller session lifecycle, engine overview. - Basis: bounded negative. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: an independent multi-agent setup composed outside the packaged app is not assessed.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: no separately evidenced whole-current system control over a portfolio of S1 units. - Disturbance / variety regulated: an individual session can run too long, fail, be stopped or require approval; these are local execution and user constraints. - Decisive decision or feedback right: the user changes permission/model/mode; deterministic runtime caps steps, retries transient failures and stops/records turns. - Decision owner: user/configuration and local runtime enforcement for a single session, not an in-boundary agent or parent making cross-unit current-control decisions. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: MAXSTEPS, /goal round and idle caps, ChatGPTController state, ToolContext permissions and session persistence. - Closure path: run limit, failure or stop → session notice/termination or retry; no global current view → changed portfolio commitment/resource decision → subsequent S1 regulation. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the model&#39;s ordinary task choices and end markers are S1 work; the limits enforce preselected bounds rather than whole-system S3 discretion. - Evidence: controller, goal bounds, permissions. - Basis: bounded negative. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: a user&#39;s oversight of many separate local conversations is outside the credited single-session governing system.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: no independent complementary audit of operational claims with a distinct corrective authority/return is evidenced. - Disturbance / variety regulated: wrong tool actions, memory-proposal errors, partial provider failures and user-visible claims may be examined by ordinary tools, user review or offline tests. - Decisive decision or feedback right: user approves individual tool actions or reviews stored memory proposals; tests and source reviews are maintainer/development activities. - Decision owner: user for ordinary approvals, same operating model for its own task, and external developers for offline quality review—not an independent runtime auditor. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: permission checks, session log and compaction invariants, memory review UI, local test suites, docs/harness-review- reports. - Closure path: approval/denial constrains a tool call, or offline tests inform developers; no complementary raw-evidence audit finding returns to a whole-system current controller. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: a review label, permission check or maintainer&#39;s code inspection is not first-party independent audit of operational reality. - Evidence: review UI and memory proposals, permissions, development-only loop review. - Basis: bounded negative. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: optional external human audit or testing may provide independent real-world assurance but is not included as an operational S3 path.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no demonstrated external-and-prospective capability-adaptation cycle feeding current operation. - Disturbance / variety regulated: model availability, provider errors, context pressure and imported user memory affect current tasks or operational settings. - Decisive decision or feedback right: user chooses an existing engine/model; ordinary assistant may plan the current request; deterministic compaction adapts context size. - Decision owner: user/configuration for provider choices, model for immediate task execution; no separate prospective adaptation decision right. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: provider adapters, memory/import tools, skills discovery, context estimation and compaction. - Closure path: engine selection, memory refresh or compacted view can change the next turn; no evidence of sensing a future external distinction, developing strategic alternatives and selecting a changed organizational capability. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: current-task research, retrying and session memory do not constitute outside-and-then intelligence under the Profile. - Evidence: README engines and local memory, loop compaction, goal current-task continuation. - Basis: bounded negative. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: source does not preclude user-led prospective research in arbitrary prompts; no durable first-party S4 feedback path is demonstrated.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no evidenced runtime identity/ultimate-policy adjudication and authoritative returned governance rule. - Disturbance / variety regulated: permissions, project trust and write risk are bounded by fixed product constraints and user approvals for ordinary actions. - Decisive decision or feedback right: developer presets permission levels; user may approve/deny tools, select project or change model; no first-party actor is tasked with adjudicating the assistant&#39;s ultimate purpose/identity. - Decision owner: developer and user for configuration and ordinary consent, not a demonstrated S5 decision owner for an identity-level matter. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: ask/code/full, Plan mode, protected paths, approval cards and system instructions. - Closure path: permission decision allows/denies a specific action; no identity-level proposal → legitimate ultimate authority → policy return governing future operation is evidenced. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: static rules and task approvals constrain S1 but do not establish a substantive S5 identity decision loop. - Evidence: permissions and approval, session mode/instructions wiring, README. - Basis: bounded negative. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: an external organization may impose identity governance on its deployment, but it is not shipped as a closed first-party mode here.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/aeaee27fd7793297205479b5d86a56826341603b/assessments/wi/wittycode.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit aeaee27fd7793297205479b5d86a56826341603b\">aeaee27 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr data-a=\"1\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-11T19:50:29+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/shalyhinpavel/go-agent-harness\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Go Agent Harness</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"1\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>1</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-11\">2026-11-11</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/shalyhinpavel/go-agent-harness/tree/362eef286581c67c8d384369d03873aad0807843\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 362eef286581c67c8d384369d03873aad0807843\">362eef2 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: convert user tasks into model-selected tool actions and completed conversational results. - Disturbance / variety regulated: user requests, tool errors/content, changed file/web observations, stream interruptions and context-window pressure. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose a registered tool and arguments after reading previous results, or end with an answer; subsequent iterations may revise the choice. - Decision owner: the configured external model acting through a first-party executable AgentEngine loop; Go infrastructure bounds execution but does not select semantic task steps. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: tool registry, provider adapters, parallel call executor, retry/compaction policy, session store, cancellation and opt-in approval. - Closure path: prompt/session → model tool call → executeToolsParallel → tool-result message persisted → next model decision or terminal answer / done chunk. - Boundary reachability: internal/bootstrap.BuildEngine wires the shipped CLI/server to NewAgentEngine, and StreamChat directly calls the configured provider and executes registered model-selected tools. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the model has task-level discretion and receives real tool feedback; provider autonomy is credited only within the packaged invocation, not as Go deterministic ownership of model judgment. - Evidence: engine turn and model loop, tool result/continuation, parallel tool executor, CLI bootstrap. - Basis: structural, first-party shipped code; confidence: high for the described model-driven single-agent loop. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: source-static examination; no live provider quality, autonomy performance or model-level identity is inferred.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: no first-party coordination relation regulating interference among distinct autonomous S1 work units is established in the standard runtime. - Disturbance / variety regulated: simultaneous calls and overlapping turns could conflict, but the shipped guards regulate one session&#39;s request lifecycle rather than negotiated interference between distinct operational agents. - Decisive decision or feedback right: BeginTurn rejects concurrent turns of one session; executeToolsParallel serializes result order and caps tool fan-out without an agent-level arbitration choice. - Decision owner: deterministic runtime for per-session exclusion/parallel tool mechanics; no distinct S2 owner or specifically composable inter-agent conflict controller shown. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: session busy map, semaphore and stable result slots. - Closure path: conflicting same-session second request returns busy/error, while independent sessions execute separately; neither is a demonstrated S1-to-S1 coordination feedback loop. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: neither model nor configurable first-party mediation is shown to select an interference-specific coordination response across independent agent work units. - Evidence: session turn guard, parallel tool result ordering, bootstrap one-engine assembly. - Basis: bounded structural absence; confidence: medium-high. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: external applications can embed several engines and implement S2, but their coordination is not supplied by this distribution.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: no whole-system current-control decision loop over a portfolio of operational commitments and common resources is shipped. - Disturbance / variety regulated: session concurrency, context exhaustion, tool side effects and model iteration budget are controlled locally, but not through a whole-current organizational intervention. - Decisive decision or feedback right: configured limits and a caller-supplied Proceed hook constrain individual turns; no first-party actor surveys current multi-S1 commitments and chooses reassignment, resource bargain or exception intervention. - Decision owner: embedding developer/operator selects configuration, runtime enforces; optional human approves individual tools (ordinary execution risk, not whole-current S3). - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: MaxIterations, MaxParallelTools, context guard, session TTL, tool approval and SSE broker. - Closure path: limit/approval refusal changes or ends an individual tool/turn, not a first-party system-wide current-control decision returned to multiple S1 operations. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: neither hard guard nor generic hook carries discretionary S3 current-control ownership on its own. - Evidence: config and hooks, limits and guards, bootstrap. - Basis: structural boundary-specific negative; confidence: medium. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: a host program may build current-control policy over many engines, but that is outside this default harness&#39;s accountable operating boundary.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: no shipped complementary independent audit judgment/return path beyond normal agent/tool reporting. - Disturbance / variety regulated: false tool-success claims or hidden operational errors are possible, but event trace alone does not audit the claim. - Decisive decision or feedback right: none shown: optional trace files record steps and errors; provider conformance check tests model adapters outside a production session. - Decision owner: normal engine records traces and configured user can inspect them later; no separate first-party auditor controls independent evidence and corrective return. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: JSONL trace.Sink, stream event chunks, unit tests and live provider conformance checker. - Closure path: logs are written for inspection/replay; no included audit decision demonstrably changes subsequent session operating authority. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: model&#39;s own turn report and trace sink do not provide independent complementary judgment, and the separate checker belongs to adjacent testing. - Evidence: trace semantics and Record, provider conformance checker, bootstrap opt-in trace. - Basis: bounded negative after tracing candidate audit surfaces; confidence: medium-high. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: trace is useful primary material for an externally composed auditor, not evidence that one ships in this runtime.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no first-party outside-and-then prospective environmental option selection and capability-return loop identified. - Disturbance / variety regulated: changing models, token limits and user memory affect current requests, not strategic future viability choices. - Decisive decision or feedback right: users/developers select providers and tools; auto-compaction summarizes past history, and memory ports retrieve user data rather than develop future capability options. - Decision owner: developer/operator configures providers, prompt and memory; the model owns current task steps, not a shipped forward-looking adaptation decision process. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: PromptAugmenter, ContextCompactor, AutoCompact, memory store, MCP registration and model configuration. - Closure path: summarized history and user dossier enter a later model request; no evidenced external trend → evaluated prospective option → changed durable harness capability → S3 return path. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: operational context preservation is not strategic learning/adaptation; a possible future custom hook is not a current first-party S4 constructor. - Evidence: compaction and prompt ports, memory bootstrap, README memory design. - Basis: bounded negative after prospective-path search; confidence: medium. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: model can reason about tomorrow if a user asks it to; this is task behavior, not an included organizational adaptation loop.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no packaged identity/ultimate-policy judgment and legitimate returned decision path. - Disturbance / variety regulated: action safety, file access, tool-level approvals and user/session isolation are runtime risk policy, not ultimate organizational mission disputes. - Decisive decision or feedback right: human may approve/deny a tool call and developer can change system prompt, but neither is a shipped process for escalating and resolving mission/identity-level questions. - Decision owner: external human/developer owns application purpose; Go runtime holds enforcement boundaries, not ultimate policy authority. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Approver, ApprovalPolicy, ReadOnly, bootstrap file/bash opt-ins, per-user session identity. - Closure path: tool approval returns a particular execution answer; no first-party route takes an identity/ultimate-policy matter to legitimate parent authority and returns a governing identity decision for future operation. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: deterministic restrictions and ordinary one-off human consent are not S5, nor is a static prompt. - Evidence: tool approval API, approval execution, server approval broker. - Basis: bounded negative; confidence: medium-high. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: application authors may add higher-recursion governance, not credited without a shipped first-party mode.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/4704aac872cafd0512e5adf0e6ddc496bbbbf918/assessments/go/go-agent-harness.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 4704aac872cafd0512e5adf0e6ddc496bbbbf918\">4704aac &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr data-a=\"1\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-11T18:07:26+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/milletcode/milletcode\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">MilletCode</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"1\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>1</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-11\">2026-11-11</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/milletcode/milletcode/tree/2278a648381e3279bb1f29ed54cf48ad19c44d66\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 2278a648381e3279bb1f29ed54cf48ad19c44d66\">2278a64 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: carry out model-directed coding/dialog tasks in configured project workspaces. - Disturbance / variety regulated: changing prompts, code/tool observations, permissions, session context and test results. - Decisive decision or feedback right: the Claude Code model chooses next edits, tools and responses during a live session; users may approve/deny restricted actions. - Decision owner: first-party-launched Claude Code agent process in an enabled instance, whose model reasoning is external but whose session/approval/feedback organization is shipped by MilletCode. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: ClaudeAdapter process launcher, SessionManager, JSONL event parser, approval response handling, SSH/local runtime and WebSocket streaming. - Closure path: browser/API prompt → launched Claude CLI with project context → model/tool work → streamed event or approval → user/CLI response → continued model step or completed session. - Boundary reachability: Claude adapter is enabled by default and SessionManager::createsession resolves its configured local or SSH executable; no custom third-party framework implementation is needed beyond installing Claude Code. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: model selects content/steps and handles tool feedback; backend only starts, meters, routes and persists the session. - Evidence: Claude adapter launch/events, session management, session API. - Basis: structural shipped CLI agent mode. - Confidence: high for adapter-agent reachability, not for quality of model execution. - Caveats: no model-provider credentials or live end-to-end workflow tested; HTTP agent adapters are immature/disabled and not needed for the S1 claim.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: no evidenced first-party discretionary regulation of interfering decisions among distinct running coding agents. - Disturbance / variety regulated: two agents could touch the same source files or make incompatible changes, but no inter-agent collision resolution loop is shipped. - Decisive decision or feedback right: per-session duplicate-key overwrite/resume and resource handling decide session identity, not cross-agent work-claim arbitration. - Decision owner: absent for an S2-specific corrective choice; operators manually coordinate tasks outside this provided relation. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: composite session keys, status registry, SSH pooling, approval queue, task-specific stop/resume and notifications. - Closure path: no first-party scope conflict event → coordination verdict → changed instructions to two distinct S1 agents demonstrated. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: session identity deduplication and transport routing prevent duplicate sessions but do not attenuate conflicting independent tasks across agents. - Evidence: session manager, session API, multi-agent feature description. - Basis: bounded no-path within included session/agent/transport and project surfaces. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: an operator or external Claude agent could independently coordinate work; that is not the first-party default S2 loop.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-p\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: P - Function: oversee and intervene in currently active sessions and project commitments across the operator&#39;s MilletCode installation. - Disturbance / variety regulated: stuck/idle agents, unexpected approvals, errored sessions and competing current project work. - Decisive decision or feedback right: human operator can select projects/sessions, issue work messages, stop/resume instances, resolve pending approvals and change active execution configuration. - Decision owner: authenticated local/hosted human console operator; no model is shown prioritizing all sessions from a whole-deployment view. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: dashboard and sessions routes, persistent session store, SessionManager, status APIs, pending-control registry, idle alerts and project discovery. - Whole-system current view: /api/sessions and projects console enumerate running/idle/stopped sessions across projects and agents, with current status and approval prompts. - Current-control decision scope: operator allocates attention and instructions, intervenes in stuck sessions and changes which active coding threads continue. - Closure path: aggregated session/project state → operator selects intervention/priority → REST action (message/approve/stop/restore) → changed agent process and subsequent dashboard state. - Boundary reachability: installed browser dashboard and REST session controls are first-party supported modes, not maintainer-only developer infrastructure. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: whole-session decisions require human selection; automatic status updates and Lark notifications only report/enforce. - Evidence: sessions API, session manager, console. - Basis: direct parent-operated current-control path. - Confidence: medium-high for parent supervision; actual scheduling effectiveness untested. - Caveats: current S3 belongs to the deployed operator, not an autonomous model supervisor; a session&#39;s approval prompt alone would not prove S3 without the whole view.\">P</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: no independent first-party review of coding-agent operational claims with separate evidence access and a corrective verdict demonstrated. - Disturbance / variety regulated: coding errors/false completion reports remain possible; installation validation and permission checks address different narrower hazards. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no shipped independent code-audit actor/constructor returns a judgment that changes worker behavior. - Decision owner: human may inspect artifacts externally, but that reviewer is not a first-party complementary path in this installed system. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: session history, Git/file browser, per-action approvals and skill after-install checks. - Closure path: user can view output and decide manually; an independent audit → findings → corrective agent control loop is not supplied. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: approval prompts are permission decisions, and existence checks for installed skills do not review agent code/work independently. - Evidence: sessions UI/API, skills verification, README. - Basis: bounded absence of independent S3 audit boundary. - Confidence: medium-high for standard distribution. - Caveats: external CI or a separately hired model reviewer could be attached, but is not credited without a first-party integration.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-p\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: P - Function: select external skill packages as future coding-agent capability options and deploy the chosen adaptation across configured agent nodes. - Disturbance / variety regulated: new external skill capabilities and changing local agent requirements beyond a current user prompt. - Decisive decision or feedback right: a human operator evaluates the available official/third-party skill option and requests install/uninstall; no autonomous model has blanket authority to define future capabilities. - Decision owner: human console operator who chooses a skill and, for official packages, provides/configures the trusted download source; model-run third-party installers execute that request within bounds. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: official skill manifest, skills marketplace UI, API install queues, configured archive source/password, per-node plan, verification after install, persistent status. - External distinction: skill packages originate outside the currently installed agent session and may introduce capabilities it previously lacked, unlike session state, logs or immediate code output. - Future / prospective distinction: the operator selects a reusable capability for subsequent sessions rather than merely answering an ongoing coding turn. - Adaptation option generated: displayed available skill packages plus manually supplied third-party skill requests; choice remains human and the catalog is not independent strategic research. - Path back into current capability / S3: operator skill selection → official/third-party install API → per-agent/node skill directory update and verification → sessions using newly available installed skills. - Closure path: offered outside skill option → parent selection/approval → installation/verification → changed agent skill availability → later coding operations. - Boundary reachability: first-party skills.rs routes and Skills UI are shipped; official install requires an explicitly configured source, while third-party install requires an operator-provided request. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: software/agents may execute installation, but the prospective option and authorization are owned by the operator, so parent P rather than A. - Evidence: skills API and install verification, skill market UI, README feature description. - Basis: first-party parent-governed capability acquisition path, narrow S4. - Confidence: medium; live source availability and installed-skill usefulness not tested. - Caveats: not general environmental foresight, adaptive product strategy or autonomous skill selection; no claim official downloads work without configured URL/password.\">P</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no evidenced first-party path where the installed deployment&#39;s ultimate mission/identity is authoritatively selected and returned into operations. - Disturbance / variety regulated: credential/auth and agent allowlists constrain execution, but do not settle the ultimate purpose of the deployment. - Decisive decision or feedback right: operator can configure access, default model, approvals and skills, but no specific ultimate-identity decision circuit is shown. - Decision owner: legitimate human owner retains general authority outside the runtime; operational permissions should not be mislabeled a closed S5 function. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: config.toml, auth.mode, agents/instances, approval controls, SSH credentials and skill installation policy. - Closure path: settings change execution permissions and capacity, not an explicit identity-level mission/organizational purpose selection and return. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: no model owns policy identity, and human password/approval controls alone do not establish parent S5 by the function-first threshold. - Evidence: configuration, README configuration, approval API. - Basis: bounded no-path at installed boundary. - Confidence: medium; an operator&#39;s outside organizational identity is not denied. - Caveats: selecting a new skill as future capability is S4 P here, not itself S5 identity authority.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/70a43d08d80314385bda7c8aac6af10e0cdf8810/assessments/mi/milletcode.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 70a43d08d80314385bda7c8aac6af10e0cdf8810\">70a43d0 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr data-a=\"1\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-11T17:36:00+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/devsohm/axi\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Axi</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"1\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>1</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-11\">2026-11-11</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/devsohm/axi/tree/b1d0fe007535f1c59d08c1f06e014c4558a4862d\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision b1d0fe007535f1c59d08c1f06e014c4558a4862d\">b1d0fe0 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Complete a submitted task through model-selected actions and committed feedback. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Tool outcomes, model errors, context limits, interruptions and partially complete work. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Select the next supported tool and its arguments, revise the action after tool observations, or return an answer. - Decision owner: The model bound to the packaged GenerationTask owns semantic choices. The harness owns phase execution and persistence. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Model collection, first-party provider adapters, ToolTask, registered tool factories, scheduler, views and checkpointed storage. - Closure path: Conversation submit → GenerationTask selects request → model calls a tool → ToolTask records result → next generation consumes result or commits completion. - Boundary reachability: Harness.open, root submission, GenerationTask and ToolTask are public supported distribution paths. The shipped provider can be configured in place of the tutorial faux provider. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The host selects provider and tool policy. The model makes per-task decisions through the supplied standard executor without a host-authored control loop. - Evidence: packages/durable/docs/tutorial.md, packages/durable/src/harness/harness.ts, packages/durable/src/harness/generation.ts, packages/durable/src/harness/generation.ts, packages/durable/src/tools/index.ts, packages/ai/src/api/openai-responses.ts - Basis: Pinned first-party source and declared distribution. - Confidence: High for the bounded structural finding. - Caveats: Source-static assessment. Provider behavior and real-world quality were not tested.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: No autonomous coordination between distinct S1 units is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Parallel conversations and file mutations can share resources. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Resolve inter-conversation interference and change subsequent operating choices across the units. - Decision owner: Scheduler and mutation queue serialize work under fixed rules. The host defines any inter-conversation policy. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Ownership graph, task scheduler, document transactions and canonical-file mutation queue. - Closure path: Locks and task ordering regulate writes and resource access. Results return to the originating tool or conversation. - Boundary reachability: Child conversations, task ownership and file tools are present. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The implemented lifecycle and write serialization do not expose a distinct cross-S1 decision/feedback role. - Evidence: packages/durable/src/harness/scheduler.ts, packages/durable/src/harness/ownership.ts, packages/durable/src/tools/file-mutation-queue.ts - Basis: Pinned first-party source and declared distribution. - Confidence: High for the bounded structural finding. - Caveats: Source-static assessment. Provider behavior and real-world quality were not tested.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: No whole-system current-control decision mode is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Live tasks, stalled phases, conversation usage and cancellation requests. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Reallocate or change whole-system operating commitments with returned discretionary intervention. - Decision owner: The app chooses configuration. The scheduler manages present phases under fixed lifecycle rules. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Task graph, views, checkpoints, metadata telemetry and cancellation. - Closure path: Current statuses drive scheduling, restart and cancellation within the lifecycle contract. - Boundary reachability: The public Harness exposes task inspection and conversation control primitives. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Observations and isolated operator controls leave the whole-system judgment and return to adopter code. - Evidence: packages/durable/src/harness/harness.ts, packages/durable/src/harness/scheduler.ts, packages/durable/src/harness/task-graph.ts, packages/durable/src/harness/view.ts - Basis: Pinned first-party source and declared distribution. - Confidence: High for the bounded structural finding. - Caveats: Source-static assessment. Provider behavior and real-world quality were not tested.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: No complementary operational audit is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: An agent response or tool outcome may disagree with task reality. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Inspect alternative evidence and return a claim-specific corrective judgment. - Decision owner: Producing conversation receives committed tool results. Hosts own any telemetry or extra hook logic. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Recorded tool phases, transcript, durable telemetry and development test fixtures. - Closure path: Runtime records return to the same conversation or host subscribers. There is no audit verdict in standard generation settlement. - Boundary reachability: Packaged telemetry and task views expose event observations. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Neither a hook nor a second arbitrary conversation carries audit-specific independence and return by default. - Evidence: packages/durable/src/harness/generation.ts, packages/durable/src/harness/telemetry.ts, packages/durable/src/harness/registry.ts, packages/durable/README.md - Basis: Pinned first-party source and declared distribution. - Confidence: High for the bounded structural finding. - Caveats: Source-static assessment. Provider behavior and real-world quality were not tested.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: No prospective adaptation loop is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Changes in model catalogs, context length or available extensions may affect future tasks. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Sense outside change, judge a future capability option and feed it into present operations. - Decision owner: Host selects model configuration and extensions. Compaction maintains the active conversation context. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Model catalogs, compaction tasks, configuration and persisted context boundaries. - Closure path: The compaction output changes the next prompt. It does not commit a prospective capability adaptation. - Boundary reachability: Compaction is a supported conversation function. Catalog tooling belongs to model package maintenance. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Selecting or summarizing context for current work does not assign outside-and-then ownership. - Evidence: packages/durable/src/harness/compaction.ts, packages/durable/src/harness/agent.ts, packages/ai/src/model-catalog.ts, packages/durable/docs/spec.md - Basis: Pinned first-party source and declared distribution. - Confidence: High for the bounded structural finding. - Caveats: Source-static assessment. Provider behavior and real-world quality were not tested.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: No ultimate identity/policy decision process is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Host policy, request identity overrides and conversation-level settings. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Resolve an identity-level dispute and apply the authoritative result to future operation. - Decision owner: The embedding application supplies policy and identity fields. Harness applies runtime configuration. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Request identity, extension registry, AgentDoc settings and ownership checks. - Closure path: Configured identity and tool policy influence requests and run admission under predefined rules. - Boundary reachability: Harness.open and conversation configure are supported configuration paths. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: An attribution string, permission boundary or configuration override does not substantiate ultimate authority arbitration. - Evidence: packages/durable/README.md, packages/durable/src/harness/harness.ts, packages/durable/src/harness/agent.ts, packages/durable/src/harness/ownership.ts - Basis: Pinned first-party source and declared distribution. - Confidence: High for the bounded structural finding. - Caveats: Source-static assessment. Provider behavior and real-world quality were not tested.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/09960790f90ed113da880c0571998635cbf1927e/assessments/ax/axi.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 09960790f90ed113da880c0571998635cbf1927e\">0996079 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr data-a=\"1\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-11T17:22:17+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/Andy1890/ruhui-agent\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">RuHui Agent</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"1\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>1</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-11\">2026-11-11</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/Andy1890/ruhui-agent/tree/4bbff9f3094f5024edb7e25ba36b4c31d55df7d6\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 4bbff9f3094f5024edb7e25ba36b4c31d55df7d6\">4bbff9f &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Carry out a local user task through iterative model-selected tool actions. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Tool failures, malformed output, incomplete workspace information and consumption limits. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Choose the next supported tool and its arguments, revise after observations, or finish with text. - Decision owner: The model actor bound to the first-party CLI/SDK turn owns the semantic choices. The runtime owns dispatch and fixed limits. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Provider adapter, context assembly, tool mode policy, local handlers, rollout and budget checks. - Closure path: Task → provider response → tool call → local observation or corrective hint → next provider step → completion or bounded stop. - Boundary reachability: CLI delegates to the SDK and the SDK binds the configured provider and executor before calling the core loop. The OpenAI-compatible adapter is an implemented mode. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The semantic action is selected from task and results by the model. The scripted provider is a fixture mode and does not substantiate agent ownership by itself. - Evidence: packages/cli/ruhui.mjs, packages/sdk/agent-node.mjs, packages/core/src/loop.ts, packages/providers/openai-node.mjs - Basis: Pinned first-party source and declared distribution. - Confidence: High for the bounded structural finding. - Caveats: Source-static assessment. Provider behavior and real-world quality were not tested.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: No distinct S1 coordination is established in the local turn boundary. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Tool order and per-workspace state can affect the current task. - Decisive decision or feedback right: An inter-operation conflict judgment would require independently operating S1 units sharing a relevant disturbance. - Decision owner: The active turn owns its tool decisions. The runtime sequences calls and keeps session state. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Serial tool dispatch, tool-batch concurrency flags and local rollout/session boundaries. - Closure path: Tool results return to the same turn. No lateral conflict signal is returned between S1s. - Boundary reachability: The CLI and SDK bind the same single-turn execution path. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Ordering tools issued by one model is internal to S1. - Evidence: packages/core/src/loop.ts, packages/tools/src/runtime.ts, packages/sdk/agent-node.mjs - Basis: Pinned first-party source and declared distribution. - Confidence: High for the bounded structural finding. - Caveats: Source-static assessment. Provider behavior and real-world quality were not tested.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: No separate current-control process over whole-system commitments is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Current turn cost, time and repeated tool failures. - Decisive decision or feedback right: A portfolio-level intervention would require a current view and discretion over commitments beyond the turn. - Decision owner: The local constructor defines budgets and stopping rules. The user initiates or resumes work. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Consumption counters, failure caps, exit states and the CLI doctor. - Closure path: Counters and observations produce fixed continuation or stopping behavior for the current turn. - Boundary reachability: SDK run/resume and doctor are reachable. Their current scope is local session execution and readiness. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Local budget guards execute configured rules inside S1. - Evidence: packages/core/src/loop.ts, packages/core/src/budget.ts, packages/sdk/agent-node.mjs - Basis: Pinned first-party source and declared distribution. - Confidence: High for the bounded structural finding. - Caveats: Source-static assessment. Provider behavior and real-world quality were not tested.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: No complementary operational audit is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Potential disagreement between a task claim and actual workspace or tool outcomes. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Independently sample evidence for the claim and return a corrective judgment. - Decision owner: The producing turn receives its own tool results. Development checks are maintained separately from task operation. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Rollout events, deterministic rollout checks and eval fixtures. - Closure path: Runtime feedback goes back to the original model. Offline checks have no first-party return into current task control. - Boundary reachability: The product exposes its own event stream. The evaluation scripts are repository development commands. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Observation and deterministic checking do not establish a separate claim-specific challenge and return in the shipped operating mode. - Evidence: packages/core/src/loop.ts, packages/sdk/agent-node.mjs, scripts/eval.mjs, scripts/data/rollout.mjs - Basis: Pinned first-party source and declared distribution. - Confidence: High for the bounded structural finding. - Caveats: Source-static assessment. Provider behavior and real-world quality were not tested.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: No prospective capability adaptation is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Workspace context and immediate changes to task inputs. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Observe external change, generate a future option and return it into current capability. - Decision owner: The user chooses workspace instructions and configuration. The active model addresses the current task. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Context discovery, configuration loading and session continuation. - Closure path: The context is assembled for the next local turn without an outside-and-then adaptation path. - Boundary reachability: Workspace and home configuration are loaded in supported modes. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Task plan updates and context assembly do not govern future capability. - Evidence: packages/core/src/context.ts, packages/sdk/agent-node.mjs, packages/tools/src/plan.ts - Basis: Pinned first-party source and declared distribution. - Confidence: High for the bounded structural finding. - Caveats: Source-static assessment. Provider behavior and real-world quality were not tested.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: No ultimate identity or policy arbitration process is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Local tool permission and instruction constraints. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Resolve a conflict in governing identity or ultimate policy and change operating commitments. - Decision owner: The embedding caller or user configures the runtime, and deterministic tool policy applies the selected mode. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Tool policy, local configuration, user-input result and sandboxed workspace helpers. - Closure path: Configured rules gate the current tool invocation. No first-party identity arbitration precedes those constraints. - Boundary reachability: The first-party dispatcher applies mode policy. The user can respond to task-level requests. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Per-action permission and external configuration do not constitute a sourced ultimate-policy decision mode. - Evidence: packages/tools/src/policy.ts, packages/tools/src/runtime.ts, packages/sdk/agent-node.mjs, schema/config.schema.json - Basis: Pinned first-party source and declared distribution. - Confidence: High for the bounded structural finding. - Caveats: Source-static assessment. Provider behavior and real-world quality were not tested.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/3de8817a771caf386c6598c21360f1d64c98eca8/assessments/ru/ruhui-agent.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 3de8817a771caf386c6598c21360f1d64c98eca8\">3de8817 &#8599;</a></td></tr>"];
